= Module 2 — Communication, Resource Sharing in IT and Information Security: Describing and Classifying Networks
**Weeks 2–4 · Lessons 7–16**
**Category of action:** Description of computer networking; classification of computer networks; network design strategies; configuration of network topology based on the physical connection of computers.
**Context:** Learners move from what a network is and why it is complex (Lessons 7–8), through classification by coverage and choosing networks for organisations (10–11), to logical architecture (13) and physical topologies (14–15). Integration activities at Lessons 9, 12 and 16 apply each block to a real organisation.

# Lesson 7: Fundamentals of Computer Networks

> OBJ:
  - Describe a computer network.
  - Compare networked computers with stand-alone systems.
  - Identify real-world examples of computer networks (e.g., home Wi-Fi, school labs, the internet) versus standalone computer setups.

## Lesson outline (1 period, 50 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Unplug the lab's switch: "What can you still do? What can't you?" | Observe; list. |
| Core 1 | 15 min | Define network; elements (nodes/hosts, links/media, devices, protocols, NOS/services); purposes (communication, resource sharing – files, printers, internet, storage, software; centralised management; collaboration); data flow basics (sender, medium, receiver, message, protocol). | Label a diagram. |
| Core 2 | 15 min | Stand-alone vs networked: comparison table (sharing, communication, cost, security, dependence, maintenance, performance, backup); when stand-alone is still appropriate (secure/offline systems, single-user kiosks). | Complete comparison. |
| Core 3 | 10 min | Examples: home Wi-Fi (router + phones + TV), school lab (switch + server + PCs + printer), cybercafé, bank branch WAN, MTN mobile network, the internet; stand-alone: a shop's offline till, exam-hall PC, industrial controller, a laptop with Wi-Fi off. | Classify photos. |
| Conclusion | 5 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 7.1 What a computer network is

> DEF:
  A **computer network** is a set of two or more computing devices (**nodes**) connected by **transmission media** (cables or wireless links) and governed by common **protocols**, so that they can **communicate and share resources** (data, files, printers, storage, internet access, applications).

| Element | Role | Examples |
| **Nodes / hosts** | Devices that send/receive data | PCs, laptops, phones, servers, printers, IP cameras, IoT sensors |
| **Transmission media** | Carry signals | UTP cable, fibre, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, 4G/5G, satellite |
| **Network devices** | Connect, direct and extend traffic | Switch, router, access point, modem, hub, repeater |
| **Protocols** | Rules for communication (format, addressing, timing, error handling) | TCP/IP, HTTP, Wi-Fi 802.11, Ethernet |
| **Network operating system / services** | Manage users, sharing and security | Windows Server, Linux, file/print/DHCP/DNS services |
| **Addresses** | Identify devices | IP address (192.168.1.10), MAC address |

Purposes: **communication** (e-mail, messaging, video calls), **resource sharing** (files, printers, scanners, internet connection, storage, software licences), **centralised data and management** (one database, backups, user accounts, updates), **collaboration** (shared documents, LMS), **reliability** (redundant paths and copies), **cost saving** (one printer for 40 PCs), **access to services** (web, cloud, e-government).

### 7.2 Networked versus stand-alone systems

> DEF:
  A **stand-alone computer** works independently – it is not connected to any other computer or network; all resources (storage, printer, software) must be attached directly to it.

| Criterion | Stand-alone system | Networked system |
| Resource sharing | None – each machine needs its own printer, files copied by USB | Shared printers, files, storage, internet, applications |
| Communication | None (sneakernet) | Instant messaging, e-mail, video, collaboration |
| Data management | Scattered copies, inconsistent versions | Central storage, single source of truth, central backup |
| Administration | Each machine configured and updated separately | Central user accounts, policies, updates, monitoring |
| Cost | Cheaper for one machine; duplicated peripherals overall | Network hardware and skills needed, but shared resources cut total cost |
| Security | Isolated from network attacks; physical risk only | Exposed to malware, hacking, misuse – needs firewalls, accounts, policies |
| Reliability/dependence | Failure affects one user; no dependence on others | Server/link failure can affect everyone; but redundancy and backups possible |
| Performance | No network congestion | Shared bandwidth can slow; but access to powerful servers/cloud |
| Scalability | Adding users means adding full machines | Easy to add nodes and services |
| Typical use | Exam PCs, isolated control systems, tills without connectivity, personal offline work | Homes, schools, offices, banks, ISPs, the internet |

### 7.3 Real-world examples

| Networked | Description | Stand-alone |
| **Home Wi-Fi** | Router/modem from Camtel/MTN links phones, laptop, smart TV and printer; shares internet and media | A laptop used offline with Wi-Fi disabled |
| **School computer lab** | 40 PCs connected by switch to a server (files, LMS), shared printer and internet via router | An exam-hall PC deliberately isolated |
| **Cybercafé** | PCs on a switch with a shared internet line and print server | – |
| **Office / bank branch** | LAN per branch linked by WAN to headquarters data centre; ATMs | An offline cash till in a small shop |
| **Hospital** | Patient-record server, imaging network, Wi-Fi for tablets | A stand-alone ultrasound machine printing directly |
| **Mobile network** | MTN/Orange cells, core network and internet gateway serving millions | A basic phone in airplane mode |
| **Industrial plant** | Sensors and PLCs on an industrial network | An air-gapped safety controller |
| **The internet** | The global network of networks interconnecting billions of devices | – |

> EXAMPLE:
  **Before and after networking a Bamenda secretariat.** Before: five stand-alone PCs, one printer moved by cable, documents on USB sticks (three infected in a term), no shared internet, each PC updated separately. After a small LAN (switch, router, shared printer, a NAS for files): printing from any desk, one internet subscription shared, nightly backup of the NAS, a shared "Templates" folder, and antivirus updated centrally – but also a need for user accounts and a firewall since all machines are now exposed together.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 7.1 – Anatomy of a small network**: PCs, laptop, phone, printer, server connected to a switch, switch to router/modem, router to the internet cloud; labels for nodes, media, devices, protocol arrows, IP addresses. **Figure 7.2 – Stand-alone vs networked**: left, three isolated PCs each with its own printer and USB stick; right, the same PCs sharing one printer, one internet line and one file server.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "A computer with internet is stand-alone if not on a LAN." Any connection to another system makes it networked. "Networks only mean the internet." A LAN works fully without internet access.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Network detectives (25 min).** (1) Walk the lab: identify and photograph/sketch nodes, media, devices; trace the path from a PC to the internet. (2) Classify 12 photo cards as networked or stand-alone and justify. (3) Debate in pairs: "Our exam centre should keep stand-alone PCs" – list two arguments each way using the comparison table.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Describe a computer network and name its five essential elements.
  2. State four purposes of networking with an example of each.
  3. Compare networked computers with stand-alone systems on sharing, administration, security and reliability.
  4. Give three real-world examples of computer networks and two of stand-alone set-ups, explaining why each is classified so.
  5. When might a stand-alone system be preferred? Give two situations.
  **Homework:** Sketch and label the network at your home or a nearby business (or describe a stand-alone set-up) and list three resources that are or could be shared.

---

# Lesson 8: Network Complexity and Operational Challenges

> OBJ:
  - Identify and discuss the benefits and limitations of networks.
  - Explain the complexity involved in setting up and maintaining data communication networks.
  - Explain the complexity of interoperability between heterogeneous systems.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 6 min | Story: the school network went down during online GCE registration – causes brainstorm. | Suggest causes. |
| Core 1 | 24 min | Benefits (sharing, communication, central management, cost, collaboration, scalability, reliability via redundancy, access to services) and limitations (cost of equipment/skills, security threats, dependence/single points of failure, performance degradation, maintenance burden, privacy, malware spread, complexity, power/connectivity in Cameroon). | Benefit/limitation grid with examples. |
| Core 2 | 35 min | Complexity of set-up and maintenance: planning (requirements, design, budget), physical layer (cabling standards, distances, power, environment), addressing and configuration (IP schemes, DHCP, DNS, VLANs), security (firewalls, accounts, updates), scalability, monitoring and troubleshooting (layered approach, tools), documentation, skills and staffing, vendor support, cost of downtime, change management; case walk-through. | Complexity map. |
| Core 3 | 27 min | Interoperability of heterogeneous systems: different hardware, OS (Windows/Linux/macOS/Android/iOS), vendors, protocols, data formats, versions, character sets, security models, legacy systems; why it is hard; solutions – open standards (TCP/IP, HTTP, SMB, PDF, Unicode), middleware/APIs, gateways/protocol converters, virtualisation, cloud services, standards bodies (IETF, IEEE, ISO, W3C, ITU). | Case: mixed school lab. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 8.1 Benefits and limitations of networks

| Benefits | Explanation / example |
| **Resource sharing** | One printer, one internet line, shared storage and software for many users – lower cost per user |
| **Communication & collaboration** | E-mail, chat, video conferencing, shared documents in real time |
| **Centralised data & management** | Single database, central backups, user accounts and policies, remote updates and monitoring |
| **Reliability & redundancy** | Multiple paths, mirrored servers, backups mean a fault need not stop work |
| **Scalability & flexibility** | Add users/devices/services incrementally; remote and mobile access |
| **Access to services** | Web, cloud applications, e-government, e-learning, online banking |
| **Speed of information** | Instant transfer instead of physical transport |

| Limitations | Explanation / example |
| **Cost** | Equipment (switches, routers, cabling, servers), software licences, skilled staff, ongoing subscriptions |
| **Security threats** | Hacking, malware spreading across all nodes, eavesdropping, insider misuse – demands firewalls, antivirus, policies |
| **Dependence & single points of failure** | Server, switch, ISP or power failure can halt everyone (online registration example) |
| **Performance degradation** | Congestion when many users share bandwidth; latency on WAN links |
| **Maintenance & management burden** | Updates, monitoring, troubleshooting, user support, documentation |
| **Privacy concerns** | Central logs and shared storage expose personal data; monitoring of users |
| **Complexity** | Many interacting components and vendors; specialist knowledge |
| **Cameroon-specific constraints** | Unreliable electricity, expensive and variable bandwidth, lightning damage, dust/heat, scarcity of technicians outside cities |

### 8.2 Complexity of setting up and maintaining data communication networks

| Dimension | What makes it complex |
| **Requirements & design** | Understanding users, applications, growth, budget; choosing type, topology, architecture, equipment; capacity planning; redundancy decisions; documentation |
| **Physical infrastructure** | Structured cabling standards (Cat 6, 100 m limit, bend radius), trunking, patch panels, racks, cooling, dust, lightning/surge protection, UPS/generators, fibre for long runs, Wi-Fi surveys (walls, interference, channels) |
| **Addressing & configuration** | IP address plans and subnets, DHCP scopes, DNS, gateways, VLANs to separate staff/learners/guests, Wi-Fi SSIDs and security, printer and server configuration, consistent naming |
| **Security** | Firewalls and rules, user accounts and permissions, authentication (RADIUS/802.1X), encryption, updates/patching, antivirus, guest isolation, physical security, policies and training – a moving target |
| **Services** | File/print servers, NOS, e-mail, web filtering, backup systems, LMS – each with dependencies |
| **Scalability & change** | Adding buildings/users, new applications (video), new devices (BYOD, IoT); change control to avoid breaking things |
| **Monitoring & troubleshooting** | Faults may lie in cable, device, configuration, ISP or software; need layered diagnosis (ping, traceroute, link lights, logs), monitoring tools, spare parts |
| **People & skills** | Trained administrators, user support, vendor contracts, documentation to survive staff turnover |
| **Cost & downtime** | Budgeting for replacement cycles (5–7 years), subscriptions, the cost of downtime during critical operations |
| **Regulation & compliance** | Data protection (Law 2024/017), lawful interception rules, acceptable-use policies |

Structured approach: **plan → design → procure → install → configure → test → document → operate → monitor → review**, with a maintenance schedule (preventive checks, firmware updates, backup verification, capacity review).

### 8.3 Interoperability between heterogeneous systems

> DEF:
  **Interoperability** is the ability of different systems, devices, applications and organisations to **exchange data and use it meaningfully**. **Heterogeneous systems** differ in hardware, operating system, vendor, protocol, data format or version.

| Source of heterogeneity | Interoperability challenge | Solution |
| **Operating systems** (Windows, Linux, macOS, Android, iOS, ChromeOS) | Different file systems, sharing protocols, permissions, drivers | Common protocols – **SMB/CIFS** (Samba on Linux), NFS, IPP printing, WebDAV; cross-platform apps and web interfaces |
| **Hardware & vendors** | Proprietary features, management interfaces, cables/connectors | **Open standards** (IEEE 802.3 Ethernet, 802.11 Wi-Fi, USB, Bluetooth); vendor-neutral management (SNMP) |
| **Protocols & versions** | IPv4 vs IPv6, old vs new TLS, Wi-Fi generations, legacy devices | Dual-stack, gateways/protocol converters, backward compatibility, phased upgrades |
| **Data formats** | .docx vs .odt, Excel vs CSV, proprietary databases, image formats, character encodings (accents!) | Open formats (**PDF, ODF, CSV, XML, JSON, Unicode UTF-8**), converters, import/export filters |
| **Applications & databases** | Systems that cannot talk to each other (school MIS vs accounting vs LMS) | **APIs**, middleware, integration platforms, standard schemas |
| **Security models** | Different authentication systems and policies | Single sign-on, directory services (LDAP/Active Directory), federation |
| **Legacy systems** | Old equipment/software still in use (Windows 7 PCs, old printers) | Emulation/virtualisation, isolated VLANs, gateways, planned replacement |
| **Cloud vs on-premises** | Mixed local and cloud services | Standard web protocols (HTTPS, REST), identity federation |

Why it matters: without interoperability data is re-typed (errors), devices are duplicated, vendor lock-in raises cost, and collaboration fails. Standards bodies that make interoperability possible: **IETF** (internet protocols – RFCs), **IEEE** (Ethernet, Wi-Fi), **ISO/IEC** (OSI, document formats), **W3C** (web standards), **ITU** (telecom), Wi-Fi Alliance, Bluetooth SIG.

> EXAMPLE:
  **The mixed lab at Lycée Bilingue de Buea.** Twenty donated Windows 10 PCs, ten Ubuntu machines, teachers' MacBooks and Android phones, a 2012 HP printer and a new school-management web app. Problems: Ubuntu PCs cannot see Windows shares; the printer has no macOS driver; the accountant's Excel macros fail in LibreOffice; accented names appear as "Ngu?bi" in the exported CSV. Solutions: a Samba file server with LDAP accounts for all OSes; a print server using IPP (AirPrint) for the old printer; files exchanged in PDF/ODF/CSV UTF-8 with the web app's API used for imports; the legacy printer placed on its own VLAN. Interoperability was mostly standards and planning, not new purchases.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 8.1 – Benefits vs limitations balance** with icons. **Figure 8.2 – Layers of network complexity**: a stacked diagram from physical (cabling, power) → addressing/configuration → security → services → people/process → compliance, each with a "what can go wrong" note. **Figure 8.3 – Interoperability hub**: Windows, Linux, macOS, Android, legacy printer and cloud app around a hub labelled "open standards (TCP/IP, SMB, IPP, PDF, UTF-8, APIs)".

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Once a network is installed it just works." Networks need continuous maintenance, updates and monitoring. "Buying everything from one vendor removes interoperability problems." It creates lock-in and still leaves phones, cloud and legacy devices to integrate.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Complexity clinic (45 min, groups).** (1) Given a scenario (school with two blocks, 60 PCs, Wi-Fi for staff, guest access, unreliable power), list ten complexity factors and one mitigation each. (2) Incident analysis: "internet down during registration" – build a fault tree (power, ISP, router, DNS, switch, cable, malware) and a troubleshooting order. (3) Interoperability challenge: five devices/OS cards + three data-format cards – propose standards and services to make them work together; identify one legacy device to isolate.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Identify and discuss four benefits and four limitations of computer networks.
  2. Explain five factors that make setting up and maintaining a data communication network complex.
  3. Why can a network be a single point of failure? How is this risk reduced?
  4. Define interoperability and heterogeneous systems; give three sources of heterogeneity.
  5. Explain how open standards, APIs and gateways help heterogeneous systems interoperate; give one example of each.
  6. Name three standards bodies and what they standardise.
  **Homework:** Interview a technician or cybercafé owner about the hardest part of keeping their network running; write half a page linking their answers to the complexity dimensions and one interoperability issue.

---

# Lesson 9: Integration Activities — Networking a Real Organisation: Description and Challenges

> ACTIVITY:
  **Context.** A local organisation (choose: a primary school with 12 donated PCs; a health centre with 3 PCs and a printer; a cooperative office with 6 mixed Windows/Linux computers; a family compound with phones, a laptop and a smart TV) currently uses stand-alone machines and asks your team whether to build a network.
  **Task (pairs, 1–2 periods).**
  1. **Describe** (Lesson 7) the current stand-alone situation and the proposed network: nodes, media, devices, protocols and services, in a labelled diagram.
  2. **Compare** networked vs stand-alone for this organisation using at least six criteria and conclude with a recommendation.
  3. **Analyse** (Lesson 8) the benefits and limitations specific to the organisation, five set-up/maintenance complexity factors with mitigations (power, security, skills, cost, scalability), and two interoperability issues with standards-based solutions.
  4. **Deliver** a two-page advisory note (word-processed, with diagram and tables) and a 4-minute briefing.
  **Criteria.** Accurate network description; balanced, criteria-based comparison; realistic complexity and interoperability analysis; clarity of recommendation and presentation.

## Rubric

| Criterion | 4 – Excellent | 3 – Good | 2 – Developing | 1 – Beginning |
| Network description & diagram | Complete, correct elements and services | Minor omissions | Partial | Incorrect |
| Comparison & recommendation | ≥ 6 criteria, evidence-based conclusion | 4–5 criteria | Few criteria | Unsupported |
| Complexity & interoperability | Five specific factors with mitigations; two interoperability solutions using standards | Mostly specific | Generic | Missing |
| Communication | Clear note and briefing | Clear | Basic | Weak |

