# Lesson 10: Types of Networks by Coverage Area

> OBJ:
  - Describe types of networks (LAN, MAN, WAN, CAN, PAN).
  - Classify properly based on coverage a given network (e.g., home Wi-Fi setup, a university).
  - Compare the network types in terms of coverage area, typical speed, cost, and number of devices/users supported.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Show a Bluetooth earphone, the lab, the University of Yaoundé I map, a Douala map and a world map. "Networks at what scale?" | Guess names. |
| Core 1 | 40 min | PAN (Bluetooth, USB tethering, NFC; ~10 m), LAN (room/building; Ethernet/Wi-Fi; 100 Mbps–10 Gbps; WLAN), CAN (campus – several buildings linked by fibre; university, hospital, industrial site), MAN (city – Camtel fibre metro ring, municipal Wi-Fi, cable TV networks; 5–50 km), WAN (country/world – leased lines, MPLS, satellite, the internet); plus notes on WLAN, SAN, VPN as overlay, GAN. Technologies, ownership, examples. | Type cards. |
| Core 2 | 25 min | Classification practice: home Wi-Fi (LAN with PAN devices), a university with 12 buildings (CAN), bank with branches nationwide (WAN), council Wi-Fi across Limbe (MAN), phone + smartwatch (PAN), the internet (WAN/GAN); ambiguous cases. | Classify 15 scenarios. |
| Core 3 | 22 min | Comparison table: coverage, speed, cost, devices/users, ownership, media, latency, examples; graph of scale vs speed/cost. | Complete and discuss. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 10.1 Network types by coverage

| Type | Coverage | Description | Technologies / media | Examples |
| **PAN – Personal Area Network** | A few metres (≈ 1–10 m) around one person | Connects personal devices | **Bluetooth**, USB, NFC, infrared, Zigbee; WPAN | Phone + earbuds + smartwatch; laptop + wireless mouse; phone tethering |
| **LAN – Local Area Network** | Room, floor or building (up to a few hundred metres) | Privately owned, high speed, low latency | **Ethernet** (UTP Cat 5e/6, 100 Mbps–10 Gbps), **Wi-Fi** (WLAN 802.11, 100 Mbps–several Gbps), switches, one or more access points | Home Wi-Fi, school computer lab, office, cybercafé |
| **CAN – Campus Area Network** | Several buildings on one site (≈ 1–5 km) | Interconnects multiple LANs owned by one organisation | Fibre backbone between buildings, core switches, campus Wi-Fi | University of Buea campus, hospital complex, SONARA refinery, military base, a large lycée with several blocks |
| **MAN – Metropolitan Area Network** | A city or metropolitan area (≈ 5–50 km) | Links LANs/CANs across a city; often operated by a telecom or municipality | Fibre metro rings, microwave links, WiMAX/4G, cable TV infrastructure | Camtel/Douala metro fibre; municipal Wi-Fi; a bank's branches within Yaoundé; cable-TV data network |
| **WAN – Wide Area Network** | Country, continent or world (> 50 km) | Connects distant LANs/MANs; uses leased or public telecom infrastructure | Leased lines, MPLS, fibre backbones (WACS/SAT-3 submarine cables landing at Kribi/Limbe), satellite (VSAT, Starlink), 4G/5G, VPN over internet | Bank network across Cameroon, MTN core network, CEMAC interbank, **the internet** (largest WAN/GAN) |
| Related terms | – | **WLAN** (wireless LAN), **SAN** (storage area network in data centres), **VPN** (secure logical network over a WAN), **GAN** (global) | | |

### 10.2 Classifying a given network

Ask: **How far apart are the nodes, who owns the infrastructure, and what links them?**

| Scenario | Classification | Reasoning |
| Home: router, two phones, laptop, smart TV; phone paired to earbuds | **LAN** (WLAN) containing **PANs** | Single dwelling; Bluetooth pairing is personal-scale |
| University with 12 buildings linked by fibre, each with its own LAN | **CAN** | Several buildings, one site, one owner |
| Bank with 40 branches across Cameroon linked to Douala HQ | **WAN** | National distances over telecom links |
| Council-run Wi-Fi covering Limbe town centre and offices | **MAN** | City-wide |
| A cybercafé with 15 PCs | **LAN** | One room |
| Two campuses in Yaoundé (Ngoa-Ekelle and Soa) linked by leased fibre | **MAN** | Same metropolitan area, different sites |
| Farmers' cooperative offices in Bafoussam and Dschang sharing a server via VPN | **WAN** (VPN over internet) | Different towns |
| Smartwatch, phone and wireless headset | **PAN** | Personal devices within metres |
| The internet | **WAN/GAN** | Global |

### 10.3 Comparing network types

| Criterion | PAN | LAN | CAN | MAN | WAN |
| **Coverage** | 1–10 m | Up to ~1 km (building) | 1–5 km (site) | 5–50 km (city) | > 50 km to global |
| **Typical speed** | 1–50 Mbps (Bluetooth), up to Gbps (USB/Wi-Fi Direct) | 100 Mbps–10 Gbps | 1–100 Gbps backbone | 100 Mbps–10 Gbps | 1 Mbps–100 Gbps per link; varies widely; internet home links 5–100 Mbps in Cameroon |
| **Latency** | Very low | Very low (< 1 ms) | Low | Low–moderate | Higher (10–600 ms; satellite highest) |
| **Cost** | Very low | Low–moderate | Moderate–high (fibre trenching) | High (telecom leases) | Very high (leased lines, international bandwidth); often pay-per-month |
| **Devices/users** | 1–8 devices | 2–~1,000 | Thousands | Thousands–hundreds of thousands | Millions–billions |
| **Ownership** | Individual | Private organisation | Single organisation | Telecom/municipality or large organisation | Telecoms/ISPs/consortia; the internet – no single owner |
| **Media** | Bluetooth, USB, NFC | UTP, fibre, Wi-Fi | Fibre backbone, Wi-Fi | Fibre, microwave, cellular | Fibre, submarine cable, satellite, cellular |
| **Error rate / reliability** | Low | Low; fully controlled | Low | Moderate | Higher; depends on carriers |
| **Examples** | Earbuds + phone | Lab, home, office | University, hospital | City fibre ring | Bank network, internet |

Trend: as coverage grows, speed per user and control **decrease** while cost, latency and complexity **increase**.

> EXAMPLE:
  **University of Douala.** Each faculty building has a LAN (switches, Wi-Fi). A fibre backbone joins buildings into a **CAN**. A leased link to the IUT campus across town creates a **MAN** segment. The connection to the RENATER-style national research network and to the internet is the **WAN**. A lecturer's laptop, phone and Bluetooth mouse form a **PAN** on top of it all – five network types in one morning.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 10.1 – Concentric coverage diagram**: PAN (person) inside LAN (building) inside CAN (campus) inside MAN (city) inside WAN (globe), with typical distances, speeds and example icons. **Figure 10.2 – Scale vs speed/cost graph**: x-axis coverage, two curves (speed per user falling, cost rising). **Figure 10.3 – Cameroon map** with submarine cable landings (Kribi, Limbe), national fibre backbone, a metro ring in Douala and a campus inset.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Wi-Fi is a WAN because it is wireless." Wi-Fi is a LAN technology; wireless WANs use cellular or satellite. "The internet is a very large LAN." It is a network of networks spanning WAN links.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Classify and compare (35 min).** (1) 15 scenario cards → PAN/LAN/CAN/MAN/WAN with justification; identify combinations. (2) Measure: use the lab's LAN speed (file transfer or iperf/LAN speed test) vs internet speed test – relate to the comparison table. (3) Build a physical concentric model on the classroom floor with string and labels; each learner places a device card at the right ring.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Describe PAN, LAN, CAN, MAN and WAN with coverage and one example of each.
  2. Classify: a home Wi-Fi with phones and a Bluetooth speaker; a university with eight buildings; a supermarket chain across Cameroon; a town-wide council network. Justify.
  3. Compare LAN and WAN on coverage, speed, cost, users and ownership.
  4. Why does latency increase with coverage? Which type has the lowest cost per device?
  5. Which network types does a lecturer use when e-mailing from a campus Wi-Fi laptop with a Bluetooth mouse to a colleague abroad?
  **Homework:** Identify five networks you use in a week and classify each, with coverage estimate, technology and owner in a table.

---

# Lesson 11: Choosing the Right Network for an Organisation

> OBJ:
  - Identify the networking needs of different types of organisations.
  - Select the most appropriate network type for a given organisational scenario.
  - Evaluate the key factors involved in choosing one network type over another for a specific organization's needs.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 6 min | Two clients: a tailoring shop with two PCs and a microfinance with 20 branches – same network? | React. |
| Core 1 | 30 min | Needs analysis: organisation types (small business, school, hospital, bank, university, government office, NGO, farm cooperative, home office) and their needs – users/devices, sites, applications (file sharing, POS, database, VoIP, video, LMS, telemedicine), bandwidth, availability, security/compliance, mobility, growth, budget, skills, power/connectivity context; requirement-gathering questions. | Needs profile for two organisations. |
| Core 2 | 30 min | Selecting the type: decision tree (one room → LAN; several buildings → CAN; city sites → MAN or VPN over WAN; national → WAN/VPN; personal devices → PAN); combining types; wired vs wireless within type; cloud vs on-premises services; scenario practice with justification. | Solve five scenarios. |
| Core 3 | 26 min | Evaluating factors: cost (capital vs operating), performance, scalability, reliability/availability, security, manageability, geography, existing infrastructure, regulatory, vendor/ISP availability in Cameroon; weighted decision matrix; trade-offs. | Weighted matrix for a case. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 11.1 Networking needs of different organisations

| Organisation | Typical users/sites | Key applications | Critical needs |
| **Small business / shop** | 2–10 users, one site | Invoicing, POS, e-mail, mobile money, printing | Low cost, simplicity, internet sharing, basic security |
| **Secondary school** | 50–2,000 learners, staff; one campus, several blocks | Computer lab, LMS, admin/MIS, Wi-Fi for staff, e-library, exam registration | Content filtering, learner/staff separation, affordability, robustness, power backup |
| **University** | Thousands; multiple buildings/campuses | E-learning, research (high bandwidth), library, student portal, Wi-Fi everywhere, labs | High capacity backbone, campus Wi-Fi, authentication (eduroam), scalability, inter-campus links |
| **Hospital / health centre** | Dozens–hundreds; wards, labs, pharmacy | Patient records, imaging (large files), telemedicine, lab systems, Wi-Fi for tablets | High availability, strict security/privacy, low latency, redundancy, isolation of medical devices |
| **Bank / microfinance** | Many branches nationwide, ATMs | Core banking (real-time), ATMs, mobile money, e-mail, video | Very high security and availability, encrypted WAN, redundancy, compliance (COBAC) |
| **Government office / council** | Several offices, public counters | E-government platforms, records, e-mail, public Wi-Fi | Security, reliability, interoperability with national systems, citizen access |
| **NGO / cooperative** | Small teams in several towns, field agents | Cloud collaboration, data collection on phones, VoIP | Low cost, mobility, works on 4G, cloud-based |
| **Manufacturing / plant** | Office + factory floor | ERP, machine control (OT), CCTV, sensors | Segregation of industrial network, reliability, environmental robustness |
| **Home office / freelancer** | 1–4 users | Internet, video calls, cloud, printing, streaming | Cheap, simple, adequate bandwidth |

Requirement-gathering questions: How many users/devices now and in five years? How many sites and how far apart? Which applications and their bandwidth/latency needs? Required uptime? Sensitive data and regulations? Mobility (Wi-Fi, remote work)? Budget (initial and monthly)? Available power, ISPs and technicians? Existing equipment?

### 11.2 Selecting the appropriate network type

| Scenario | Recommended type(s) | Justification |
| Tailoring shop, 2 PCs + printer + phones, one room | **LAN** (small Wi-Fi router with Ethernet ports) + PAN for phone/printer Bluetooth | Single room, low cost, shared internet/printer |
| Lycée with 4 blocks 200 m apart, lab + admin + staff Wi-Fi | **CAN** (fibre or outdoor point-to-point Wi-Fi between blocks, LAN per block) | Several buildings, one site; VLANs for learners/staff/admin |
| Microfinance with HQ in Bafoussam and 20 branches in the West Region | **WAN** – VPN over ISP links (4G/fibre) with MPLS or leased line for HQ; LAN per branch | Distant sites, real-time secure transactions |
| Hospital campus with 6 buildings | **CAN** with redundant fibre backbone, medical-device VLANs | Availability and isolation |
| Council offices across Limbe + public Wi-Fi hotspots | **MAN** (municipal fibre/microwave) | City scale, own infrastructure |
| NGO with 5 field offices in different regions | **WAN via internet + cloud** (Google Workspace, VPN); small LANs/4G routers per office | Low budget, mobility |
| University with 2 campuses in the same city | **CAN** per campus + **MAN** link (leased fibre) + WAN to the internet/research networks | Scale and geography |
| Freelancer at home | **LAN** (Wi-Fi) + PAN | Simplicity |

Within the type, also decide: **wired vs wireless** (speed/security vs mobility/cost), **on-premises servers vs cloud** (control vs low upkeep), **ISP technology** (fibre, 4G/5G, VSAT for remote areas), redundancy (two ISPs, UPS).

### 11.3 Evaluating the key factors

| Factor | Questions | Influence on choice |
| **Cost** | Capital (equipment, cabling, installation) and operating (subscriptions, power, staff, maintenance); total cost of ownership over 5 years | Small budgets favour LAN + cloud + 4G; WAN leases are costly |
| **Performance** | Required bandwidth and latency per application (video/imaging vs e-mail) | Fibre CAN/MAN for heavy data; avoid satellite for real-time |
| **Scalability** | Growth in users, sites and services | Choose modular switches, structured cabling, scalable IP plan |
| **Reliability / availability** | Tolerable downtime; redundancy needs | Banks/hospitals need redundant links and power |
| **Security & compliance** | Data sensitivity, regulations (Law 2024/017, banking rules) | Encrypted WAN/VPN, segmentation, private links |
| **Geography & distance** | Number and distance of sites, terrain, right of way for cables | Determines LAN/CAN/MAN/WAN and media (fibre vs wireless) |
| **Existing infrastructure & ISP availability** | Fibre in the street? 4G coverage? Ducts/buildings? | Reuse; VSAT where nothing else |
| **Manageability & skills** | In-house staff or outsourced? | Simple, cloud-managed equipment where skills are scarce |
| **Mobility & user experience** | BYOD, field agents, guests | Wi-Fi, 4G routers, VPN clients |
| **Environment & power** | Outages, lightning, dust, heat | UPS, surge protection, solar, ruggedised outdoor links |
| **Time to deploy** | Urgency | Wireless links faster than trenching fibre |

**Weighted decision matrix:** list options (e.g., fibre CAN vs wireless bridges vs 4G routers per block), weight factors (cost 30 %, performance 25 %, reliability 20 %, security 15 %, scalability 10 %), score each 1–5, multiply and total; discuss trade-offs rather than only the number.

> EXAMPLE:
  **Case: "Espoir" microfinance.** Needs: real-time transactions at 20 branches, high security, 99.5 % uptime, limited technicians outside Bafoussam. Options: (A) MPLS leased lines to every branch – excellent but 3 million XAF/month; (B) VPN over dual 4G/fibre internet per branch with cloud-managed routers – 700,000 XAF/month, encrypted, redundant; (C) VSAT everywhere – costly, high latency. Weighted matrix favours B with MPLS only for HQ and the data centre, plus a UPS in each branch – the "right" network balances factors, it is not the biggest.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 11.1 – Needs-to-network decision tree**: sites/distance → LAN/CAN/MAN/WAN; then branches for wired/wireless, cloud/on-premises, redundancy. **Figure 11.2 – Weighted decision matrix** example filled for the microfinance case. **Figure 11.3 – Organisation gallery**: shop, school, hospital, bank, NGO, each with its network type badge and top-two critical factors.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Bigger/faster is always better." Over-provisioning wastes scarce budget; match to needs and growth. "Wireless is free." Outdoor links need masts, licences (for some bands), power and maintenance.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Consultancy challenge (45 min, groups).** Each group draws an organisation card with needs details. Produce: a needs profile (users, sites, applications, constraints), the selected network type(s) with a sketch, a weighted decision matrix comparing two implementation options, and a two-minute recommendation. Peer panel scores using the factor list; whole class discusses trade-offs that differed between groups.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Identify the networking needs of a secondary school, a hospital and a microfinance institution (three each).
  2. Select and justify the most appropriate network type for: a 3-block lycée; a supermarket chain in five cities; a home office; council offices across a town.
  3. Evaluate five key factors in choosing a network type and explain a trade-off between two of them.
  4. What is a weighted decision matrix and why is it useful?
  5. Why might an NGO choose cloud services over an on-premises server?
  **Homework:** Write a one-page network recommendation for an organisation you know (church, clinic, shop): needs, chosen type, factors evaluated, rough monthly cost.

---

# Lesson 12: Integration Activities — Classifying and Choosing Networks for Organisations

> ACTIVITY:
  **Context.** The Divisional Delegation of Secondary Education plans to connect three institutions: a lycée with five blocks; a bilingual college with one building; and the Delegation office in town, 6 km away – and wants staff phones and tablets to work everywhere.
  **Task (groups of 3–4, 2 periods).**
  1. **Classify** (Lesson 10) every network segment involved (PAN for personal devices, LAN per building, CAN for the lycée, MAN/WAN link to the Delegation, WAN to the internet) in a labelled map with coverage, expected speed and media.
  2. **Compare** the segments in a table (coverage, speed, cost, users, ownership).
  3. **Select** (Lesson 11) the appropriate type and implementation for each institution and the inter-site link using a needs profile and a weighted decision matrix (at least two options for the 6 km link: leased fibre, microwave/point-to-point wireless, VPN over 4G/fibre internet).
  4. **Evaluate** the key factors and constraints (budget, power, ISP availability, security of learner data, scalability for exam periods).
  5. **Deliver** a three-page proposal with diagrams, tables and a cost estimate, plus a 5-minute presentation.
  **Criteria.** Correct classification of all segments; accurate comparison; justified selection with matrix; realistic evaluation of factors; quality of documents and presentation.

## Rubric

| Criterion | 4 – Excellent | 3 – Good | 2 – Developing | 1 – Beginning |
| Classification & map | All segments correctly classified with coverage/speed/media | Minor errors | Several errors | Incorrect |
| Comparison table | Complete and accurate | Mostly accurate | Partial | Missing |
| Selection & decision matrix | Well-justified, ≥ 2 options weighed | Justified | Weak justification | Unjustified |
| Factor evaluation | Realistic, context-specific, trade-offs explained | Adequate | Generic | Absent |
| Proposal & presentation | Professional, clear, within time | Clear | Basic | Weak |

