# Lesson 19: Selecting Equipment for Organisational Networks

> OBJ:
  - Select appropriate network equipment for specific organizational needs (e.g., a school).
  - Explain the factors to consider when selecting network components.
  - Plan and design a network setup suitable for an organization's size and purpose.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Two quotes for a school network: 8 million vs 2.5 million XAF – how to judge? | Discuss. |
| Core 1 | 30 min | Selection factors: number of users/devices and growth, required speed (Gigabit, Wi-Fi 6), coverage and building layout, PoE needs, managed features (VLANs, QoS), reliability (redundant PSU, warranty), security features, compatibility/standards, scalability, budget and TCO, power consumption/UPS, vendor support and local availability, ease of management (cloud-managed), environment (heat, dust). Spec-sheet reading (ports, backplane, SFP, PoE budget, Wi-Fi standard, throughput). | Decode two datasheets. |
| Core 2 | 35 min | Selecting for a school (worked example): per-block switches, core switch, APs count (coverage + density: ~25–30 devices per AP), router/firewall with filtering, server or cloud, cabling grades, UPS sizing, printers, monitoring; alternatives for clinic and small business. | Fill selection table. |
| Core 3 | 22 min | Planning and design method: requirements → site survey → logical design (VLANs, IP plan) → physical design (topology, cable schedule, rack) → equipment list & budget → implementation phases → testing/acceptance → documentation; deliverables. | Draft a design brief. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 19.1 Factors to consider when selecting network components

| Factor | Questions | Example decision |
| **Capacity: users/devices & growth** | How many wired ports and Wi-Fi clients now/in 3–5 years? | 40 PCs → 48-port switch (20 % spare) |
| **Performance** | Required speeds; applications (video, imaging, LMS); uplink bandwidth | Gigabit access ports, 10 G/SFP+ uplinks; Wi-Fi 6 APs |
| **Coverage & layout** | Building materials, distances, floors, outdoor links | AP per 2–3 classrooms; fibre for > 100 m; wireless bridge for the boarding house |
| **Features** | VLANs, QoS, PoE, link aggregation, spanning tree, content filtering, VPN, guest portal, DHCP/DNS | Managed PoE switches; firewall with web filtering for learners |
| **Reliability & availability** | Redundant power supplies, MTBF, warranty, spare units, UPS run-time | UPS 30 min for racks; spare switch |
| **Security** | Port security, 802.1X, WPA3, firewall depth, firmware updates | Enterprise APs with WPA2/3-Enterprise for staff |
| **Compatibility & standards** | IEEE 802.3/802.11 compliance, interoperability with existing gear, open standards | Avoid proprietary lock-in |
| **Scalability & modularity** | Stackable switches, adding APs under one controller, extra SFP slots | Cloud/controller-managed Wi-Fi |
| **Cost & TCO** | Purchase + licences + power + maintenance + replacement cycle | Refurbished enterprise vs new consumer gear |
| **Power & environment** | Consumption (PoE budget), heat, dust, lightning; solar compatibility | Fanless switches in dusty rooms; surge arrestors on outdoor links |
| **Manageability & skills** | Local technicians' skills; remote/cloud management; monitoring | Simple GUIs (UniFi, MikroTik, TP-Link Omada) where staff are few |
| **Vendor support & availability** | Local suppliers in Douala/Yaoundé, spare parts, warranty service, documentation | Choose brands with local presence |
| **Regulatory** | ART licensing for outdoor radio bands, data protection | Use licence-free 5 GHz responsibly |

### 19.2 Selecting equipment for a school (worked example)

Scenario: 2 blocks + admin, 60 lab PCs, 80 staff devices on Wi-Fi, 1,200 learners (limited Wi-Fi in library), internet 50 Mbps fibre + 4G backup, 24 classrooms.

| Need | Selected equipment | Specification & justification |
| Lab connectivity (2 × 30 PCs) | 2 × 48-port Gigabit managed switches | Spare ports; VLAN "LAB"; 4 × SFP uplinks |
| Admin block (10 PCs, server, printers, APs) | 1 × 24-port Gigabit **PoE+** managed switch | Powers APs/cameras; VLAN "ADMIN" |
| Core | 1 × layer-3 switch with 10 G SFP+ ports (or router-on-a-stick for small budgets) | Inter-VLAN routing; fibre uplinks to blocks |
| Inter-block links | Single-mode fibre (2 pairs) between blocks; 150 m boarding house: 5 GHz point-to-point bridge | Distance > 100 m; lightning isolation |
| Wi-Fi | 10 × Wi-Fi 6 dual-band ceiling APs (staffroom, admin, library, 1 per 2–3 classrooms in pilot), controller/cloud-managed | ~25–30 devices per AP; SSIDs Staff (WPA2-Enterprise/strong PSK), Learners (portal, time limits), Guest (isolated) |
| Internet edge | Router/firewall (MikroTik/pfSense/Fortinet) with dual WAN, content filtering, bandwidth control per VLAN | Safeguarding, fair share, failover to 4G |
| Modem | Fibre ONT (ISP) + 4G LTE router as backup | Redundancy |
| Servers / NOS | 1 × server (Xeon, 32 GB RAM, RAID-1 SSD, 2 × 4 TB) running Ubuntu Server/Windows Server: authentication (AD/LDAP), file/print, DHCP/DNS, LMS (Moodle) or cloud Google Workspace for Education | Central accounts and data; backup NAS + cloud |
| Cabling | Cat 6 structured cabling, patch panels, wall outlets, labelled; racks with cable management | 25-year infrastructure; ≤ 90 m runs |
| Power protection | 1 × 3 kVA UPS (server room), 1 × 1 kVA per block rack, surge protectors | ENEO outages; lightning season |
| Printing | 2 × network laser printers with print server/queues | Shared, quota-managed |
| Monitoring & tools | Controller dashboard, SNMP monitoring (LibreNMS/Zabbix), cable tester, spare switch and APs | Fast fault detection |
| Optional | IP cameras (PoE), IP phones, digital signage | Later phases |

Alternatives: **health centre** – add medical-device VLAN, higher redundancy, UPS run-time, strict firewall; **small business** – wireless router with integrated switch, one AP, cloud services, NAS for backup.

### 19.3 Planning and designing a network set-up

| Phase | Deliverable |
| **1. Requirements** | Purpose, users/devices (now/future), applications and bandwidth, sites/buildings, security/compliance, budget, timeline, constraints (power, ISPs) |
| **2. Site survey** | Floor plans, distances, cable routes, existing infrastructure, Wi-Fi survey (walls, interference), power points, rack locations, environmental risks |
| **3. Logical design** | Architecture (client/server, cloud), VLANs (Admin, Staff, Lab, Learners, Guest, Devices), IP addressing plan (e.g. 10.10.VLAN.0/24), DHCP/DNS, naming, security policy (firewall rules, filtering, authentication), services |
| **4. Physical design** | Topology per level (star access, tree campus), equipment placement, cable schedule (from–to, length, type), rack layout, Wi-Fi AP map, inter-building links, power/UPS |
| **5. Equipment list & budget** | Bill of materials with specifications, quantities, unit/total costs, licences, installation labour, spares, contingency (10 %) |
| **6. Implementation plan** | Phases (core + admin first, labs, Wi-Fi, boarding), schedule, responsibilities, risk plan, acceptance tests |
| **7. Testing & acceptance** | Cable certification, connectivity per outlet, throughput, Wi-Fi coverage checks, failover test, security tests, user acceptance |
| **8. Documentation & handover** | Diagrams (logical/physical), IP/VLAN tables, configurations backup, passwords vault, maintenance schedule, training |

Design rules of thumb: 20–25 % spare switch ports; one AP per 25–30 concurrent devices; UTP runs ≤ 90 m + patch cords; Gigabit to desks, 10 G uplinks where possible; separate learner and admin traffic; every rack on UPS; two internet paths for critical sites; label everything.

> EXAMPLE:
  **Two quotes decoded.** Quote A (8 M XAF): Cisco Catalyst switches with 5-year support, Wi-Fi 6E APs everywhere, dedicated firewall, structured cabling certified – high TCO justification for a large private school. Quote B (2.5 M XAF): TP-Link/MikroTik managed switches, UniFi APs in key zones, MikroTik router with filtering, cabling by a local technician, refurbished server – meets requirements of a government lycée with growth path. The ICT club evaluated both against the factor list: B chosen, with an insistence on Cat 6 cabling, PoE budget adequacy, UPS in every rack and a 10 % spare-parts line.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 19.1 – Factor wheel** for equipment selection with icons and the question for each. **Figure 19.2 – Annotated datasheet**: switch spec sheet with ports, PoE budget, switching capacity, SFP, management features highlighted and explained. **Figure 19.3 – School network design**: logical diagram (VLANs/IP plan) beside physical diagram (blocks, racks, fibre, APs, UPS), plus bill-of-materials table.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Consumer Wi-Fi routers are enough for a school." They cannot handle client density, VLANs or centralised management. "The cheapest quote saves money." Poor cabling and unmanaged gear cost more in downtime and replacement.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Design studio (50 min, groups).** Each group receives an organisation brief and floor plan (school, clinic, council office, hotel). Produce: requirements summary; logical design (VLAN/IP table); physical design sketch (topology, racks, APs, links); equipment list with specifications and quantities (from a supplied price list) within budget; phased plan; three risks with mitigations. Present the bill of materials and defend two selection decisions using the factor list.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain eight factors to consider when selecting network components.
  2. Select and justify equipment for a school with two blocks, 60 lab PCs and staff Wi-Fi.
  3. What is PoE and why is it useful in a school network? What is a PoE budget?
  4. Distinguish managed and unmanaged switches; when is a managed switch essential?
  5. Outline the phases of planning and designing a network and name two deliverables of each.
  6. State four design rules of thumb (ports, APs, cable length, UPS).
  **Homework:** Prepare a bill of materials (equipment, spec, quantity, estimated cost, justification) for networking a 15-PC cybercafé with Wi-Fi and a printer, and a half-page implementation plan.

---

# Lesson 20: Integration Activities — From Design to a Working Network

> ACTIVITY:
  **Context.** Your ICT club has been given a room with 8 PCs, a switch, a wireless router, cables, a printer and (optionally) an old PC to act as server, to create a "Digital Corner" for the school library, which must later connect to the school network.
  **Task (groups, 2 periods).**
  1. **Equipment identification and selection** (Lessons 17, 19): inventory the available components (role, layer, specifications); list any missing items with justification and a cost estimate; explain the factors guiding your choices (ports, speed, PoE, security, UPS).
  2. **Design** (Lesson 19): requirements, logical design (workgroup or simple client/server, IP plan, share plan), physical star topology drawing with port map and cable lengths.
  3. **Set-up and test** (Lesson 18): cable and connect all PCs and the printer; configure addressing (DHCP from the router or static), hostnames and workgroup; create shared folders with correct permissions and a shared printer; connect and test from every PC (ping, share access, printing, internet if available); secure (Private profile, strong passwords, remove guest access).
  4. **Troubleshoot** two faults injected by the teacher (wrong subnet mask; disconnected uplink; sharing disabled) and document the diagnosis.
  5. **Document and hand over**: one-page network documentation (diagram, IP/hostname/share table, equipment list, test results, maintenance tips) and a 4-minute demonstration.
  **Criteria.** Correct identification and justified selection; sound design; fully working, tested, secured network; effective troubleshooting; quality of documentation and demonstration.

## Rubric

| Criterion | 4 – Excellent | 3 – Good | 2 – Developing | 1 – Beginning |
| Identification & selection | All components correctly described; missing items justified with factors | Minor gaps | Several errors | Incorrect |
| Design | Complete logical + physical design | Mostly complete | Partial | Missing |
| Set-up & testing | All tests pass, secured | Most tests pass | Partially working | Not working |
| Troubleshooting | Both faults found quickly, method documented | One fault or slow | Guessing | None |
| Documentation & demo | Professional, complete, clear | Good | Basic | Weak |

