# Lesson 33: Protocol Suites and the OSI Reference Model

> OBJ:
  - Explain the role of protocol suites in network communication.
  - Describe the seven layers of the OSI reference model and their functions.
  - Relate network protocols to the appropriate layers of the OSI reference model.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Sending a parcel abroad: writer → packer → courier → customs → plane → … each with their own rules; the parcel passes down and up layers. | Map to networking. |
| Core 1 | 20 min | Protocol suites: definition (a coordinated set of protocols working together in layers), why suites (division of labour, interoperability, modularity, independent evolution, troubleshooting), examples (TCP/IP suite, OSI protocols, AppleTalk/IPX legacy, Bluetooth stack, Zigbee); TCP/IP 4/5-layer model vs OSI 7-layer; ISO's OSI (1984) as reference for teaching/design. | Suite diagram. |
| Core 2 | 45 min | The seven OSI layers bottom-up: **Physical** (bits, media, connectors, voltages, hubs/repeaters), **Data Link** (frames, MAC addresses, error detection, media access; switches, bridges, NICs; LLC/MAC sublayers), **Network** (packets, logical IP addressing, routing; routers), **Transport** (segments, ports, reliability, flow control; TCP/UDP), **Session** (establish/manage/terminate dialogues; checkpoints; NetBIOS, RPC, SIP aspects), **Presentation** (data format, encoding, compression, encryption; ASCII/Unicode, JPEG/MPEG, TLS aspects), **Application** (network services to user apps; HTTP, SMTP, DNS, FTP). Encapsulation/decapsulation, PDUs (bits, frames, packets, segments, data), mnemonics ("Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away"; "All People Seem To Need Data Processing"), devices per layer, troubleshooting bottom-up. | Layer cards; encapsulation walk-through. |
| Core 3 | 22 min | Relating protocols to layers: mapping table (Ethernet/Wi-Fi/PPP/ARP – 1–2; IP/ICMP/IPsec/OSPF – 3; TCP/UDP – 4; NetBIOS/RPC/PPTP – 5; TLS/SSL, JPEG, MPEG, ASCII, encryption/compression – 6; HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, DHCP, SMTP, IMAP, FTP, SSH, SMB, SNMP – 7); TCP/IP mapping; devices and addresses per layer; exam-style classification. | Mapping exercise. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 33.1 Protocol suites

> DEF:
  A **protocol suite (stack)** is a **coordinated set of protocols, organised in layers, that together provide complete network communication** – each protocol handling one aspect (physical signalling, addressing, reliable delivery, application services) and passing data to the layer above or below through defined interfaces. The **TCP/IP suite** is the suite of the internet; the **OSI model** is the ISO's seven-layer **reference model** used to describe, design and compare suites.

| Why suites and layering | Explanation |
| **Division of labour** | Each layer solves one problem well (IP routes; TCP makes it reliable; HTTP formats web messages) |
| **Interoperability** | Any vendor implementing the layer standards can communicate |
| **Modularity & independent evolution** | Wi-Fi 7 replaces Wi-Fi 5 without changing HTTP; HTTP/3 changes without new cables |
| **Abstraction** | Application developers ignore cables and routes; hardware designers ignore applications |
| **Troubleshooting** | Faults isolated layer by layer (cable → IP config → DNS → application) |
| **Reuse** | One transport (TCP) serves thousands of applications |

| Suite / model | Layers | Notes |
| **OSI reference model (ISO 7498, 1984)** | 7: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application | Conceptual reference; OSI's own protocols (X.400, X.500 partially live on in LDAP/certificates) |
| **TCP/IP suite (DoD model)** | 4: Network Access (Link), Internet, Transport, Application (5-layer variant splits Physical/Data Link) | The suite actually running the internet; OSI layers 5–7 merged into Application |
| Legacy suites | IPX/SPX (Novell), AppleTalk, NetBEUI, SNA | Replaced by TCP/IP |
| Specialised stacks | Bluetooth, Zigbee/Thread, cellular (LTE/5G) stacks, industrial (Modbus, PROFINET) | Own lower layers; often carry IP |

### 33.2 The seven layers of the OSI model

| Layer | Name | Function | PDU | Addressing | Devices | Example protocols/standards |
| **7** | **Application** | Provides **network services directly to user applications** (web, e-mail, file transfer, name resolution, remote access); defines message formats and dialogues for services | Data | URLs, e-mail addresses (application-level) | Gateways, proxies, application firewalls, servers | HTTP/HTTPS, DNS, DHCP, SMTP/POP3/IMAP, FTP/SFTP, SSH, Telnet, SMB, SNMP, NTP, SIP |
| **6** | **Presentation** | **Translates data formats** between application and network: character encoding (ASCII, Unicode/UTF-8), data structures, **compression** (gzip, JPEG, MPEG), **encryption/decryption** (TLS/SSL functions) | Data | – | – | TLS/SSL (often placed 5–6), ASCII, UTF-8, JPEG, PNG, MPEG, MP3, XML/JSON encodings, ASN.1 |
| **5** | **Session** | **Establishes, manages, synchronises and terminates sessions (dialogues)** between applications; checkpoints and recovery; dialogue control (half/full duplex); authentication of sessions | Data | Session IDs | – | NetBIOS, RPC, PPTP/L2TP (tunnel sessions), SIP (call sessions), SOCKS, session aspects of TLS/HTTP cookies |
| **4** | **Transport** | **End-to-end delivery between processes**: **segmentation/reassembly**, **port numbers**, **reliability** (TCP: ACKs, retransmission, ordering), **flow and congestion control**, or fast connectionless service (UDP) | **Segment** (TCP) / Datagram (UDP) | **Port numbers** (80, 443, 53, 25…) | Firewalls (stateful), load balancers | **TCP, UDP**, SCTP, QUIC |
| **3** | **Network** | **Logical addressing and routing of packets across multiple networks**; path determination, fragmentation, TTL | **Packet** | **IP addresses** (IPv4/IPv6) | **Routers**, layer-3 switches | **IP** (v4/v6), ICMP, IPsec, ARP (border with L2), routing protocols OSPF/BGP/RIP |
| **2** | **Data Link** | Reliable transfer of **frames** across a **single link**: physical (MAC) addressing, framing, **error detection (CRC)**, **media access control** (CSMA/CD, CSMA/CA), flow control on the link; sublayers **LLC** (802.2) and **MAC** | **Frame** | **MAC addresses** (48-bit) | **Switches**, bridges, NICs, access points | **Ethernet (802.3)**, **Wi-Fi (802.11)**, PPP, HDLC, Frame Relay, ATM, VLAN tagging (802.1Q), STP |
| **1** | **Physical** | Transmission of **raw bits** over the medium: electrical/optical/radio **signals**, voltages, bit timing, connectors, pinouts, cable specs, modulation, topology at the wire level, duplex | **Bits** | – | Hubs, repeaters, cables, connectors, transceivers, modems (partly), media converters | RJ-45/Cat 6 (TIA-568), fibre standards, 100BASE-TX/1000BASE-T signalling, DSL, USB physical layer, Bluetooth/Wi-Fi radio layer, RS-232 |

**Encapsulation:** sending host passes data down – each layer adds its header (and Data Link a trailer/CRC): Data → Segment (TCP header with ports) → Packet (IP header with addresses) → Frame (Ethernet header with MACs + FCS) → Bits. The receiver **decapsulates** upward. Intermediate devices operate up to their layer: a switch reads frames (L2), a router packets (L3), a firewall segments/applications (L4–7).

**Mnemonics:** bottom-up "**P**lease **D**o **N**ot **T**hrow **S**ausage **P**izza **A**way"; top-down "**A**ll **P**eople **S**eem **T**o **N**eed **D**ata **P**rocessing".

**TCP/IP mapping:** OSI 5–7 → Application; 4 → Transport; 3 → Internet; 1–2 → Network Access (Link + Physical).

### 33.3 Relating protocols to OSI layers

| Protocol / standard | OSI layer | Reason |
| HTTP, HTTPS (application semantics), DNS, DHCP, SMTP, POP3, IMAP, FTP, SFTP, SSH, Telnet, SMB, NFS, SNMP, NTP, LDAP | **7 – Application** | Provide services to applications |
| TLS/SSL (encryption/format), JPEG, GIF, PNG, MPEG, MP3, ASCII, Unicode, gzip, XML/JSON encoding | **6 – Presentation** | Data representation, compression, encryption |
| NetBIOS, RPC, PPTP, L2TP (session set-up), SIP (dialogue), SOCKS, session management of TLS | **5 – Session** | Dialogue establishment/management |
| TCP, UDP, SCTP, QUIC (transport functions) | **4 – Transport** | End-to-end, ports, reliability |
| IPv4, IPv6, ICMP, IPsec, IGMP, OSPF, BGP, RIP | **3 – Network** | Logical addressing/routing |
| Ethernet (802.3), Wi-Fi (802.11 MAC), PPP, HDLC, ARP (L2/L3 boundary), VLAN 802.1Q, STP, Bluetooth link layer, MAC addresses | **2 – Data Link** | Framing, MAC, link error detection |
| Cat 5e/6 UTP & RJ-45 (TIA-568), fibre (1000BASE-LX), 100BASE-TX signalling, DSL modulation, radio frequencies of Wi-Fi/Bluetooth/4G, USB electrical spec, hubs/repeaters | **1 – Physical** | Bits and signals on media |

Address/device summary: Layer 1 – no address (hub/cable); Layer 2 – MAC (switch/AP/NIC); Layer 3 – IP (router); Layer 4 – port (stateful firewall); Layer 7 – URL/hostname (proxy/web server). Troubleshooting **bottom-up**: link light (L1) → `ipconfig`/ARP (L2–3) → `ping` gateway/`tracert` (L3) → `telnet host 443`/port checks (L4) → browser/DNS/application errors (L5–7).

> EXAMPLE:
  **A WhatsApp message through the layers.** L7: WhatsApp's messaging protocol formats "On arrive à 8h"; L6: text encoded UTF-8 and encrypted end-to-end (Signal protocol); L5: the chat session with the server is maintained/resumed; L4: TCP (or QUIC/UDP) segments with ports; L3: IP packets routed from the phone through MTN's core to WhatsApp servers; L2: LTE/Wi-Fi frames with MAC/radio link addressing and CRC; L1: radio waves at 1.8 GHz (LTE) or 2.4/5 GHz (Wi-Fi). At the recipient, the layers unwrap in reverse. When the message shows one grey tick, troubleshooting climbs from L1 (signal bars) to L7 (server status).

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 33.1 – The OSI ladder**: seven coloured layers with function, PDU, address, device and protocol examples per layer, and the TCP/IP four layers aligned alongside. **Figure 33.2 – Encapsulation/decapsulation**: data gaining TCP, IP, Ethernet headers/trailer down the sender's stack, crossing switch (L2) and router (L3) in the middle (drawn only up to their layer), and unwrapping at the receiver. **Figure 33.3 – Protocol-to-layer map**: protocol logos/labels placed on the ladder; ARP/TLS straddling boundaries. **Figure 33.4 – Bottom-up troubleshooting flowchart** with commands per layer.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "The internet runs OSI." It runs TCP/IP; OSI is the reference model used to describe it. "Each protocol fits exactly one layer." Some span or straddle (ARP, TLS, SIP); layering is a teaching and design aid, and TCP/IP merges 5–7.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Layer relay and mapping (45 min).** (1) Human stack: seven learners per team, each a layer with a coloured envelope (header); pass a message down, across a "switch" (opens only two envelopes) and a "router" (three), and up the other side; time it; then inject faults (wrong MAC, bad CRC, missing port) and see which layer detects them. (2) Mapping game: 30 protocol/device/address cards placed on a wall ladder; justify borderline cases (ARP, TLS, modem). (3) Troubleshooting drill: for five symptoms ("no link light", "APIPA address", "ping gateway OK but no web", "certificate error", "page 404") identify the layer and the check. (4) Compare OSI and TCP/IP models in a two-column table.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain what a protocol suite is and give three reasons why network communication is organised in layers.
  2. Describe the seven OSI layers and their functions, naming the PDU and one device or address type for each.
  3. Explain encapsulation and decapsulation using a web request; which layers do a switch, a router and a stateful firewall operate at?
  4. Relate the following to OSI layers: HTTP, TLS, TCP, UDP, IP, ICMP, Ethernet, Wi-Fi radio, RJ-45, DNS, ARP, NetBIOS, JPEG, SMTP.
  5. Map the OSI layers to the TCP/IP model.
  6. Give a mnemonic for the OSI layers and describe bottom-up troubleshooting with a command per layer.
  **Homework:** Create an A4 OSI reference chart (layer, function, PDU, address, devices, ≥ 3 protocols each, TCP/IP alignment) and annotate it with the path of an e-mail through the layers.

