# Lesson 34: Integration Activities — Analysing and Optimising Data Transmission for an Organisation

> ACTIVITY:
  **Context.** A district hospital complains: video consultations with specialists in Yaoundé stutter, Wi-Fi in the wards is unreliable, the new digital X-ray machine 120 m from the server room cannot be connected with the UTP cable the electrician ran, sensors in the vaccine fridges must report temperatures around the clock, and the network printer queue "freezes" the reception PC. The board asks for a transmission analysis and improvement plan.
  **Task (groups of 3–4, 2 periods).**
  1. **Modes and access methods** (Lesson 27): identify the transmission mode (simplex/half/full-duplex) and access method (CSMA/CA, switched full-duplex) for each traffic type (video call, ward Wi-Fi, X-ray transfer, sensor reports, printing) with arrow diagrams; explain why ward Wi-Fi with 60 devices per AP stutters.
  2. **Bandwidth class and transmission types** (Lessons 28–29): classify each need as narrowband or broadband; specify serial/parallel, synchronous/asynchronous and analog/digital aspects (e.g. fridge sensors: analog → ADC → asynchronous narrowband LoRa/NB-IoT; X-ray: digital synchronous serial Gigabit/fibre).
  3. **Media selection** (Lesson 30): choose media for the X-ray link (> 100 m: fibre vs media converters vs wireless bridge), the specialist link (fibre vs 4G with QoS), ward Wi-Fi redesign (5 GHz, AP count), sensors (LPWAN) – with a criteria-based justification and cost estimate.
  4. **Peripheral control** (Lesson 31): explain the printer-queue freeze in terms of buffering/spooling and handshaking, and propose the fix (print server with spooler, correct driver, interrupt/priority note); identify polling vs event-driven monitoring for the fridge sensors.
  5. **Protocols and OSI** (Lessons 32–33): list the protocols for each service (video: SIP/RTP over UDP with QoS; X-ray: DICOM over TCP/TLS; sensors: MQTT/CoAP over UDP/TCP; printing: IPP/TCP; management: SNMP), place them on the OSI ladder, and explain how TCP/IP reliability/efficiency choices (TCP vs UDP, QoS marking, window sizes on the WAN) improve each service; include a bottom-up troubleshooting procedure for the video stutter.
  6. **Deliver** a four-page report with diagrams (signal-flow arrows, media plan, OSI mapping table) and a 6-minute presentation; if the lab allows, demonstrate a Wireshark capture distinguishing TCP and UDP flows and a duplex/speed check on a NIC.
  **Criteria.** Correct identification of modes, access methods and transmission types; sound, justified media choices; accurate explanation of peripheral control issues; correct protocol/OSI mapping and TCP/IP evaluation; quality of diagrams, report and presentation.

## Rubric

| Criterion | 4 – Excellent | 3 – Good | 2 – Developing | 1 – Beginning |
| Modes, access methods & types | All traffic correctly classified with diagrams and reasons | Minor errors | Several errors | Incorrect |
| Media selection | Criteria-based, costed, appropriate for all five needs | Appropriate, less justified | Partly appropriate | Inappropriate |
| Peripheral control analysis | Buffering/spooling/handshaking/polling correctly applied and fix proposed | Mostly correct | Superficial | Missing |
| Protocols & OSI | Complete mapping, TCP/UDP/QoS reasoning, troubleshooting procedure | Mostly complete | Partial | Absent |
| Report & presentation | Professional diagrams, clear, within time | Clear | Basic | Weak |

