# Lesson 39: Internet Services — E-mail and Information Retrieval

> OBJ:
  - Identify and describe common internet services (e-mail, WWW, FTP, VoIP, instant messaging, cloud services…).
  - Create an e-mail account and send an e-mail with an attachment.
  - Perform effective information retrieval using search engines (keywords, operators, evaluating sources).

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | "What did you do online this week?" – map to services. | Brainstorm. |
| Core 1 | 20 min | Internet services table: **WWW**, **e-mail**, **FTP/file sharing/cloud storage**, **VoIP/video calls**, **instant messaging**, streaming, social networking, e-commerce/payments, cloud computing (SaaS/PaaS/IaaS), search engines, online gaming, remote access (SSH/RDP), IoT services, newsgroups/forums, e-learning; protocols and examples per service. | Service–protocol matrix. |
| Core 2 | 40 min | E-mail in depth: how e-mail works (client → SMTP → recipient's server → IMAP/POP3 → client; webmail), address anatomy, headers (To/Cc/Bcc/Subject), attachments (size limits ~25 MB; cloud links; formats; compress), etiquette (subject line, greeting, concise, signature, reply vs reply-all, forward), folders/labels/filters, spam/phishing recognition, security (2FA, recovery, HTTPS), professional address for GCE/university applications. **Hands-on step sheet**: create a Gmail/Outlook account (or use lab accounts), set up profile & 2FA, compose and send an e-mail with an attached document and an image to the teacher and Cc a classmate, reply, forward, create a label/folder and a filter, add a signature. | Create account; send e-mail with attachment. |
| Core 3 | 27 min | Information retrieval: search engines (Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo; how indexing/ranking works), choosing **keywords** (specific nouns, synonyms, remove stop words), **operators** (quotes " ", minus –, OR, site:, filetype:, intitle:, define:, .., *; Tools ▸ time range; image/reverse search; Google Scholar), refining queries, **evaluating sources** (CRAAP: Currency, Relevance, Authority, Accuracy, Purpose; domain clues .gov.cm/.edu/.org; cross-checking; bias; AI-generated content caution), citing sources, saving/organising findings. Hands-on search challenge. | Search challenge; source evaluation. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 39.1 Common internet services

| Service | Description | Protocols / technologies | Examples |
| **World Wide Web (WWW)** | Linked hypertext pages and web applications accessed by browsers | HTTP/HTTPS, HTML/CSS/JS, URLs, DNS | News sites, portals, web apps |
| **E-mail** | Store-and-forward electronic messages with attachments to addresses user@domain | SMTP (send), IMAP/POP3 (receive), webmail over HTTPS | Gmail, Outlook.com, Yahoo, institutional mail |
| **File transfer & cloud storage** | Upload/download and synchronise files; sharing links | FTP/SFTP, HTTPS, WebDAV | Google Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox, WeTransfer, FileZilla |
| **VoIP & video calling** | Voice/video over IP instead of circuit telephony | SIP, RTP/SRTP, WebRTC | WhatsApp calls, Zoom, Meet, Teams, Skype |
| **Instant messaging & chat** | Real-time text, voice notes, media, groups | XMPP, proprietary (Signal protocol E2E encryption) | WhatsApp, Telegram, Messenger, Signal |
| **Search engines** | Index and rank web content for queries | Crawlers, HTTPS | Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo |
| **Social networking** | Profiles, sharing, communities (Lesson 38) | HTTPS APIs | Facebook, TikTok, LinkedIn |
| **Streaming media** | On-demand/live audio-video | HTTPS/DASH/HLS, RTMP | YouTube, Netflix, Spotify, Boomplay |
| **E-commerce & online payments** | Buying/selling, digital payments | HTTPS, payment APIs, USSD | Jumia, MoMo/Orange Money, PayPal |
| **Cloud computing** | On-demand computing resources: **SaaS** (apps), **PaaS** (platforms), **IaaS** (servers/storage) | HTTPS, virtualisation | Google Workspace (SaaS), Microsoft 365, AWS/Azure (IaaS) |
| **E-learning** | Courses, LMS, MOOCs | HTTPS, LMS (Moodle, Classroom) | Coursera, Khan Academy, MINESEC e-learning |
| **Remote access** | Control a distant computer/terminal | SSH, RDP, VNC, VPN | IT support, servers |
| **Online gaming** | Multiplayer games, e-sports | UDP/TCP, WebSocket | PUBG Mobile, eFootball |
| **Forums, newsgroups, wikis, blogs** | Discussion and publishing | HTTPS (legacy NNTP) | Reddit, Wikipedia, WordPress |
| **IoT & mapping services** | Connected devices; location services | MQTT, HTTPS, GPS | Google Maps, smart meters |
| **E-government & e-services** | Official procedures online (Lesson 42–43) | HTTPS | Tax filing, e-visa |

### 39.2 E-mail

> DEF:
  **Electronic mail (e-mail)** is a store-and-forward service for exchanging messages and attached files between addresses of the form **username@domain** (e.g. nadia.fotso@gmail.com). Messages travel from the sender's client to their mail server (SMTP), across the internet to the recipient's mail server (SMTP), where they wait until the recipient's client downloads (POP3) or synchronises (IMAP) them – or reads them via webmail.

| Element | Description |
| Address | `username@domain` – local part + @ + mail domain (gmail.com, minesec.gov.cm) |
| **To / Cc / Bcc** | Main recipients / copied (visible) / blind copies (hidden – use for mass mails to protect privacy) |
| **Subject** | Short, specific ("Application for Form 5 Science Club – Nadia Fotso") |
| Body | Greeting, purpose in the first lines, clear paragraphs, courteous close, **signature** (name, class/role, contact) |
| **Attachments** | Files sent with the message; limits (Gmail/Outlook ≈ 25 MB – larger via Drive/OneDrive link); prefer PDF for documents, compressed images; name files clearly; scan for viruses |
| Reply / Reply all / Forward | Reply to sender; reply to everyone (use sparingly); forward to a new recipient (remove sensitive history) |
| Folders/labels, filters, archive, search, stars | Organisation and automation (e.g. label all mail from school) |
| Spam/junk, phishing | Unsolicited or fraudulent mail – check sender address, links (hover), urgency, spelling; never send passwords/PINs; report |
| Security | Strong password, **2-step verification**, recovery phone/e-mail, HTTPS webmail, log out on shared PCs, beware attachments (.exe, macros) |
| Etiquette (netiquette) | Professional address, proper subject, no ALL CAPS, proofread, respond within a reasonable time, respect confidentiality, avoid heavy attachments |
| Webmail vs client | Browser (Gmail.com, Outlook.com) vs app (Outlook, Thunderbird, mobile Mail) configured with IMAP/SMTP |

**Step sheet – create an account and send an e-mail with an attachment (Gmail; Outlook.com similar):** 1. Go to gmail.com ▸ Create account ▸ For my personal use. 2. Enter first/last name, date of birth, gender. 3. Choose a professional username (firstname.lastname; add digits if taken); create a strong password (12+ chars, mixed) and confirm. 4. Add a recovery phone/e-mail; verify code by SMS. 5. Accept terms; skip optional offers. 6. Settings ▸ See all settings ▸ General ▸ Signature: "Nadia Fotso | Upper Sixth Science | Lycée Bilingue de Yaoundé"; Save. 7. Google Account ▸ Security ▸ 2-Step Verification ▸ turn on (phone prompt/SMS). 8. **Compose**: To = teacher's address; Cc = classmate; Subject = "ICT Lesson 39 – Practical submission – Nadia Fotso"; Body = greeting, one paragraph describing the attachment, close, signature. 9. Click the **paper-clip** ▸ select `Lesson39_report.pdf` (< 25 MB) and an image; wait for upload; (for a large file: Drive icon ▸ insert link). 10. Proofread; **Send**; open Sent to confirm. 11. Open the teacher's reply; **Reply** with a thank-you; **Forward** the original to yourself with the history trimmed. 12. Create label "ICT" and a filter (from: teacher → apply label, never spam). 13. Mark a sample phishing mail as spam; report it. 14. Sign out (shared PC).

### 39.3 Effective information retrieval

> KEY:
  A **search engine** crawls the web, indexes pages and ranks results by relevance, authority, freshness and location. Effective retrieval means choosing **precise keywords**, using **operators** to refine, and **evaluating** results critically before using or citing them.

| Technique | How | Example |
| **Keywords** | Use specific nouns and distinctive terms; drop stop words; add context (place, year, format); try synonyms and the language of experts | `cameroon GCE ICT syllabus 2026 pdf` instead of `what is the ict syllabus` |
| **Exact phrase " "** | Forces the phrase | `"transmission media" guided unguided` |
| **Exclude –** | Removes a term | `jaguar speed -car` |
| **OR / \|** | Either term | `telework OR telecommuting cameroon` |
| **site:** | Within a domain/type | `site:minesec.gov.cm calendrier`; `site:.edu machine learning` |
| **filetype:** | Specific files | `filetype:pdf OSI model tutorial` |
| **intitle: / inurl:** | Words in title/URL | `intitle:"lesson plan" networking` |
| **define:** | Definitions | `define:extranet` |
| **Number range ..** | Ranges | `laptop 200000..300000 XAF` |
| **Wildcard *** | Unknown word | `"internet was invented in *"` |
| Tools ▸ time range, language, region | Recency and locale | Past year for prices |
| **Reverse image search** | Verify images | Google Lens / TinEye |
| **Google Scholar, library databases, Wikipedia references** | Academic sources | Scholar: `"digital divide" Cameroon` |
| AI answer engines | Useful summaries; **verify** – can hallucinate; follow citations | – |

| Evaluating a source – CRAAP test | Questions | Red flags |
| **Currency** | When published/updated? Do links work? | Undated; old statistics for fast-changing topics |
| **Relevance** | Does it answer your question at the right level/context (Cameroon)? | Off-topic, foreign context assumed universal |
| **Authority** | Who wrote it? Credentials? Institution? Domain (.gov.cm, .edu, ITU, WHO)? Contact info? | Anonymous, no about page, look-alike domains |
| **Accuracy** | Evidence, references, verifiable facts, consistent with other reputable sources? Spelling/grammar? | No sources, sensational, contradicts consensus |
| **Purpose** | Inform, teach, sell, persuade, entertain? Bias? Ads? Sponsored? | Hidden agenda, clickbait, "miracle" claims |

Also: **cross-check** ≥ 2 independent sources; distinguish primary/secondary sources; note **date accessed**; cite (APA/MLA or a simple format: author, title, site, URL, date); organise with bookmarks/Zotero/notes; respect copyright/licensing (Creative Commons for images).

> EXAMPLE:
  **Researching "impact of Starlink on rural education in Cameroon".** Poor: `starlink cameroon schools good?` → blogs and ads. Better: `"Starlink" Cameroon schools OR education 2024..2026 site:.cm OR site:.org` → ministry/NGO reports; `filetype:pdf "satellite internet" Africa education ITU` → ITU study; Scholar for peer-reviewed papers; reverse-image check on a viral photo of a "Starlink classroom" reveals it was taken in Kenya. CRAAP test rejects a sensational blog (no author, 2021 data, selling kits) and keeps the ITU report and a MINPOSTEL communiqué. The e-mail to the teacher attaches the sourced summary as PDF with a proper subject line.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 39.1 – Internet services wheel** with icons and protocols. **Figure 39.2 – E-mail journey**: sender's client → SMTP server → internet → recipient's server → IMAP/POP3/webmail → recipient; labelled with ports and where TLS protects it. **Figure 39.3 – Annotated compose window**: To/Cc/Bcc, subject, body structure, attachment clip, Drive link, send. **Figure 39.4 – Search operator cheat sheet** and a **CRAAP scorecard** with a sample site scored.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "The first result is the best/true." Ranking mixes relevance, popularity and ads; evaluate. "Bcc is sneaky." It protects recipients' privacy in group mails. "An attachment that is a .zip from a known sender is safe." Accounts get hijacked – verify unexpected attachments.

> ACTIVITY:
  **E-mail and search lab (55 min).** (1) Complete the e-mail step sheet: account (or lab account), signature, 2FA, send an e-mail with a PDF and image attached to the teacher with a classmate in Cc; reply and forward; create a label and filter; identify two phishing signs in a sample. (2) Search challenge (20 min): ten questions requiring operators (e.g. "Find a PDF from a Cameroonian government site about the 2026 school calendar"; "Find the year Camtel's SAT-3 landing was inaugurated with two sources"; "Find a Creative-Commons image of a fibre cable"); record query, operator used, source URL, CRAAP score. (3) Source evaluation: score three given pages with the CRAAP rubric and justify accept/reject. (4) No-computer alternative: paper e-mail form with headers/attachment checklist; operator-matching cards; printed pages for CRAAP scoring.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Identify and describe eight common internet services, naming a protocol and an example for each.
  2. Explain how an e-mail travels from sender to recipient, naming the protocols.
  3. List the steps to create an e-mail account and send a message with an attachment; explain To, Cc and Bcc.
  4. State six rules of e-mail etiquette and four signs of a phishing e-mail.
  5. Write refined search queries using at least four operators for a given research question.
  6. Apply the CRAAP test to evaluate a source; why cross-check?
  **Homework:** Send the teacher an e-mail (proper subject, greeting, signature) attaching a one-page PDF answering a research question of your choice, with three sources evaluated using CRAAP and the exact queries/operators used; Cc a study partner.

---

# Lesson 40: Teleworking, Videoconferencing, URLs and Cookies

> OBJ:
  - Explain teleworking/telecommuting and videoconferencing and their advantages and disadvantages.
  - Describe the structure of URLs and the role of cookies in web browsing.
  - Set up and use a teleconferencing platform (e.g., Zoom, Google Meet, Microsoft Teams) for a virtual meeting.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room, webcams/phones)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Recall 2020: how did lessons, church, business continue? | Share. |
| Core 1 | 25 min | **Teleworking/telecommuting** (working from home/remote location using ICT; forms: full remote, hybrid, mobile work, freelancing; requirements: connectivity, devices, VPN/cloud, collaboration tools, policies), **videoconferencing** (real-time audio-video meetings; features: screen share, chat, recording, breakout rooms, whiteboard, polls, captions, waiting room, virtual backgrounds); advantages/disadvantages for employees, employers, society (commuting, cost, flexibility, productivity vs isolation, blurred boundaries, security, digital divide, power/connectivity in Cameroon); telemedicine, virtual classes, remote interviews. | Pros/cons matrix. |
| Core 2 | 25 min | **URL structure**: scheme (https), subdomain/host/domain/TLD (www.minesec.gov.cm; ccTLD .cm), port (:443), path (/exams/2026/results.html), query string (?class=U6&lang=en), fragment (#top); URL encoding; absolute vs relative; shorteners and risks; reading URLs to spot phishing. **Cookies**: small text key–value files stored by the browser per site; purposes (session management/login, preferences, shopping cart, analytics, **tracking/advertising**); first-party vs third-party; session vs persistent; secure/HttpOnly flags; consent banners (GDPR-style; Cameroon's 2024 personal-data law); managing cookies in browser settings; alternatives (local storage, fingerprinting). | Dissect URLs; inspect cookies (DevTools). |
| Core 3 | 37 min | **Set up a teleconferencing platform** – step sheet (Google Meet; Zoom and Teams noted; Jitsi as no-account option): create account, schedule meeting with calendar invite and link, settings (waiting room/lobby, passcode, host controls, mute on entry, recording permissions), join etiquette (camera, mute, lighting, name), run: admit participants, share screen/slides, use chat, raise hand, polls, breakout rooms, captions, record, end and share recording/notes; low-bandwidth tips (audio-only, camera off, 4G fallback, dial-in). Learners host a 10-minute mock meeting. | Host/participate in a mock meeting. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 40.1 Teleworking/telecommuting and videoconferencing

> DEF:
  **Teleworking (telecommuting)** is **performing one's job away from the traditional workplace** – at home, a co-working space or on the move – using ICT (internet, cloud tools, VPN, e-mail, videoconferencing) to communicate with colleagues and access work systems. **Videoconferencing** is a **live, two-way audio and video meeting between two or more participants at different locations** over a network, usually with screen sharing, chat and recording.

| Perspective | Advantages | Disadvantages |
| **Employees** | No commute (time, transport cost, traffic in Douala/Yaoundé); flexibility; work–life balance; access to jobs anywhere (freelancing for foreign clients in USD/EUR); inclusion for people with disabilities or caring duties | Isolation, blurred boundaries/overwork, home distractions, costs of power/data/equipment borne at home, fewer promotion signals, need for self-discipline |
| **Employers** | Lower office costs; access to wider talent; continuity during crises (COVID-19, strikes); often higher productivity for focused work; measurable outputs | Management/supervision challenges; team cohesion; **security risks** (home Wi-Fi, personal devices, phishing); onboarding harder; digital tools cost; legal/insurance questions |
| **Society/environment** | Less traffic and pollution; decentralisation (jobs outside big cities); resilience | Digital divide (rural connectivity, cost of data, electricity outages); erosion of local commerce near offices; surveillance software concerns |
| **Videoconferencing specifically** | Saves travel; records meetings; brings experts/specialists to remote schools and clinics (telemedicine, virtual classes); inclusive features (captions) | Bandwidth demands (0.5–3 Mbps per stream), latency/jitter problems; "Zoom fatigue"; technical failures; security ("Zoombombing" without waiting rooms/passcodes); reduced non-verbal communication |

Forms of telework: fully remote, **hybrid** (2–3 days), mobile work (sales, field staff), telecentres/co-working hubs (e.g. ActivSpaces Buea, Douala tech hubs), freelancing platforms. Requirements: reliable connectivity (fibre/4G + backup), a suitable device, **VPN** or cloud access to work systems (Lessons 21–22), collaboration tools (Lesson 38), security policy (MFA, encryption, updates), ergonomics and clear communication norms. Related services: telemedicine, e-learning, remote interviews, remote court hearings.

### 40.2 URLs and cookies

> DEF:
  A **URL (Uniform Resource Locator)** is the **address of a resource on the internet**, specifying the protocol (scheme), the host (domain) and the path (plus optional port, query and fragment). A **cookie** is a **small piece of text data that a website stores in your browser** and that the browser sends back to that site with later requests – used to remember sessions, preferences and, often, to track users.

```
https://www.minesec.gov.cm:443/exams/2026/results.html?class=U6&lang=en#top
 │        │   │       │   │  │   │                       │                  │
 scheme   sub domain  TLD port path                     query string       fragment
 (protocol) domain  (2nd-level + ccTLD .cm)             (parameters key=value, & separated)  (position in page)
```

| Part | Meaning | Example / notes |
| **Scheme/protocol** | How to access | `https://` (secure web), `http://`, `ftp://`, `mailto:`, `file://` |
| **Subdomain** | Sub-section/server of the domain | `www`, `mail`, `results`, `portal` |
| **Domain name** | Registered name; read right to left: **TLD** (.com, .org, .edu, .gov; country codes **.cm**, .fr, .uk), second-level (minesec.gov), further levels | Phishers use look-alikes: `minesec-gov.cm.example.ru`, `mtn-momo-bonus.com` – the real domain is the part just before the TLD |
| **Port** | Service port (usually hidden – default 80/443) | `:8080` for some intranet apps |
| **Path** | Folder/file on the server | `/exams/2026/results.html` |
| **Query string** | Parameters after `?`, separated by `&` | `?q=osi+model&hl=en` – search terms, filters, tracking IDs (utm_source) |
| **Fragment** | Position within the page after `#` | `#top`, `#section3` |
| URL encoding | Special characters as %XX | space → `%20`, é → `%C3%A9` |
| Absolute vs relative | Full URL vs path relative to the current page (used in HTML links – Lesson 46) | `images/logo.png` |
| Shorteners | bit.ly, tinyurl hide the destination | Preview before clicking; phishing risk |

| Cookie aspect | Description |
| Content | Name=value pairs (e.g. `session_id=8f3a…`, `lang=fr`, `cart=12,45`), domain, path, expiry, flags |
| **Session cookie** | Deleted when the browser closes – keeps you logged in during a visit |
| **Persistent cookie** | Stored until expiry (days–years) – "remember me", preferences, tracking |
| **First-party** | Set by the site you visit – login, cart, settings |
| **Third-party** | Set by other domains embedded in the page (ad networks, analytics, social buttons) – **cross-site tracking** and profiling; increasingly blocked by browsers |
| Legitimate roles | Session management (login), personalisation (language, theme), shopping carts, analytics (visits), security tokens (CSRF) |
| Concerns | Tracking across sites, targeted advertising, profiling; cookie theft (session hijacking) – flags `Secure` (HTTPS only) and `HttpOnly` (no script access), `SameSite` |
| Consent & law | Cookie banners (EU GDPR/ePrivacy); Cameroon's **Law No. 2024/017 on personal data protection** and ANTIC guidance require transparency and consent for personal data processing |
| Managing | Browser settings: block third-party cookies, clear on exit, site exceptions; private mode; extensions; DevTools ▸ Application ▸ Cookies to inspect |
| Alternatives/beyond cookies | Local storage, browser fingerprinting, tracking pixels, login-based tracking |

### 40.3 Setting up and using a teleconferencing platform

| Platform | Account | Free limits | Strengths |
| **Google Meet** | Google account | 60 min for 3+ participants (1:1 up to 24 h); 100 participants | Simple, browser-based, Calendar/Classroom integration, low bandwidth mode |
| **Zoom** | Zoom account (host only) | 40 min group meetings; 100 participants | Breakout rooms, polls, rich host controls, works on weak links |
| **Microsoft Teams** | Microsoft account / school 365 | 60 min; 100 participants (free) | Channels, files, assignments (Education) |
| **Jitsi Meet** | None needed (open source) | Unlimited (meet.jit.si or self-hosted) | Privacy, no account, self-hostable on a school server |
| WhatsApp video | Phone number | 32 participants | Ubiquitous, low bandwidth; few controls |

**Step sheet – Google Meet (Zoom/Teams equivalents in brackets):**
**A. Prepare (host).** 1. Sign in at meet.google.com (zoom.us / teams.microsoft.com). 2. **Schedule**: Google Calendar ▸ Create ▸ title "ICT Club – Virtual meeting", date/time, add guests (e-mails), "Add Google Meet video conferencing", description with agenda; Save & send invites (Zoom: Schedule ▸ set passcode, waiting room, mute on entry; Teams: Calendar ▸ New meeting ▸ Meeting options – lobby). 3. Meeting settings: Host management ON (only host can share/mute), Quick access OFF so guests knock (waiting room), captions language. 4. Test: Settings (gear) ▸ audio/video devices, preview, lighting (face the window), background blur, headset to avoid echo. 5. Prepare slides/documents; close unneeded apps; plug in the charger; have a phone hotspot as backup.
**B. Run the meeting.** 6. Join 5 min early; admit participants from the lobby; welcome and state agenda/rules (mute when not speaking, raise hand, use chat for questions). 7. **Present now** ▸ A tab / window / entire screen – share slides (share a Chrome tab for video with audio). 8. Use **Chat** for links, **Raise hand** queue, **Polls/Q&A**, **Breakout rooms** (Meet Workspace/Zoom/Teams) for 5-minute group tasks, **Whiteboard** (Jamboard alternatives/Zoom whiteboard). 9. **Record** (with consent; announce) – Meet Workspace/Zoom/Teams; otherwise assign a note-taker. 10. Manage: mute noisy mics, remove disruptive participants, lock the meeting (Zoom) once everyone is in. 11. Close: summarise decisions and actions, thank participants, **End call for everyone**.
**C. After.** 12. Share recording link/notes via e-mail or the class group; collect feedback (Form); store the recording in the shared Drive with restricted permissions.
**D. Participant etiquette & low bandwidth.** Join from a quiet, well-lit place; camera on when possible, mute otherwise; name displayed correctly; dress appropriately; do not share meeting links publicly; on weak 4G: turn off video/reduce to 360p, use audio-only or dial-in numbers (Zoom/Teams), close other downloads, prefer wired or sit near the router; with power cuts, keep a charged phone as fallback.

> EXAMPLE:
  **A parent–teacher conference goes hybrid at a Limbe college.** The ICT prefect schedules a Meet in Calendar (link + passcode lobby), tests audio, shares the results slides from a Chrome tab and runs a poll on Saturday classes; parents in Douala and Germany join, two on audio-only 3G. A parent reads the invitation URL carefully – `meet.google.com/abc-defg-hij` – and rejects a forwarded look-alike `meet-google.com-join.info`. Later, when the school's website asks her to accept cookies, she allows only essential cookies and blocks third-party ones; her session cookie keeps her logged into the results portal until she closes the browser.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 40.1 – Telework ecosystem**: home worker with laptop → VPN → company cloud/server; collaboration tools; hybrid calendar; pros/cons badges. **Figure 40.2 – URL anatomy diagram** with colour-coded parts and a phishing look-alike comparison. **Figure 40.3 – Cookie lifecycle**: first request → server Set-Cookie → stored → sent back; first- vs third-party illustrated with an ad network across two sites. **Figure 40.4 – Annotated Meet/Zoom interface**: mute/camera, present, chat, raise hand, participants/lobby, record, breakout rooms, captions, end; plus a "low-bandwidth checklist" card.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Cookies are viruses." They are plain text, cannot execute; the concern is privacy/tracking. "HTTPS means the site is trustworthy." It only means the connection is encrypted – phishing sites also use HTTPS; read the domain. "Recording a meeting is always allowed." Inform participants and obtain consent; store recordings securely.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Virtual meeting studio (55 min).** (1) URL dissection: label all parts of five URLs (including one with query/fragment and two phishing look-alikes); decode two URL-encoded strings. (2) Cookie inspection: open DevTools ▸ Application/Storage ▸ Cookies on a news site; list first- vs third-party cookies, session vs persistent; block third-party cookies and reload; observe. (3) Host a mock meeting: groups of five rotate as host/participants – schedule with invite, lobby, screen share of a slide, chat question, poll, 3-minute breakout, (simulated) recording, proper closing; peers score with a checklist; one round in "low-bandwidth mode" (video off). (4) Telework debate: "Cameroonian companies should adopt hybrid work" – use the pros/cons matrix and local constraints (power, data cost). (5) No-computer alternative: paper storyboard of the meeting flow and a URL/cookie worksheet.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain teleworking and videoconferencing; give three advantages and three disadvantages of each from an employee's and an employer's viewpoint.
  2. Label the parts of `https://results.gceboard.cm/2026/olevel.php?centre=1042&sort=name#list` and explain each.
  3. How can reading a URL help detect a phishing site? Give an example.
  4. Describe what cookies are, three legitimate uses and two privacy concerns; distinguish first-party/third-party and session/persistent cookies.
  5. List the steps to schedule, secure, run and close a virtual meeting on a platform of your choice, including three host controls and three low-bandwidth tips.
  6. Which Cameroonian law governs personal data and what does it imply for websites using cookies?
  **Homework:** Schedule and host a 10-minute virtual meeting with two classmates (screenshot of the invite and participant list – no personal data of others beyond names), then write a one-page report: platform settings used, problems met, and an evaluation of teleworking feasibility for a chosen Cameroonian job.

