# Lesson 41: Integration Activities — Connecting, Collaborating and Communicating Online

> ACTIVITY:
  **Context.** Your ICT club has been asked by a rural partner school (200 learners, 4G coverage, no fibre, intermittent power) to design and demonstrate a "Connected School" starter programme: how they will get online, browse safely, use social platforms responsibly, run e-mail and research, and hold virtual classes with your school.
  **Task (groups of 4, 2 periods).**
  1. **History and impact brief** (Lesson 36): a half-page introduction on why the internet matters for a Cameroonian school, with two advantages, two risks and one digital-divide measure.
  2. **Connectivity plan** (Lesson 37): compare three feasible access technologies for the partner school (4G router, FWA, LEO satellite) on speed, latency, cost (installation and monthly in XAF), availability, reliability and power; recommend one with a backup; specify a standard browser configuration (privacy settings, extensions policy, bookmarks folder) for the lab.
  3. **Digital-citizenship kit** (Lesson 38): a one-page social-media safety charter and a description of the collaborative environment (Google Workspace for Education or Microsoft 365) with sharing-permission rules; the kit itself must be produced collaboratively in a shared document with version history and comments as evidence.
  4. **Services pack** (Lesson 39): a step-by-step guide for teachers to create professional e-mail accounts, send attachments and set up labels/filters; a research skills card (keywords, five operators, CRAAP test) with a worked example on a Cameroonian topic; send the pack to the teacher by e-mail with attachments and proper etiquette.
  5. **Virtual class pilot** (Lesson 40): schedule and host a 15-minute demonstration virtual lesson linking both "schools" (two rooms or two groups), with lobby/passcode, screen sharing, chat, a poll and a low-bandwidth fallback; annotate the meeting URL and explain the cookies/consent notice on the platform; include a telework/tele-teaching feasibility note for the partner school's staff.
  6. **Deliver** a 6–8 page programme document (PDF) plus a 7-minute presentation, and a 2-minute recorded segment (or live demo) of the virtual lesson.
  **Criteria.** Accuracy and completeness across the five lessons; justified, costed connectivity choice; quality and safety of the digital-citizenship and services materials; successful, well-managed virtual meeting; evidence of genuine collaboration (version history, comments, task board); e-mail etiquette; presentation quality.

## Rubric

| Criterion | 4 – Excellent | 3 – Good | 2 – Developing | 1 – Beginning |
| Internet context & impact | Precise history/impact points, local examples, balanced | Adequate | Thin | Missing/inaccurate |
| Connectivity & browser plan | Three options compared on all criteria, costed, justified recommendation with backup; complete browser policy | Justified, minor gaps | Partial comparison | Unjustified |
| Digital citizenship & collaboration | Clear charter, correct permissions rules, strong collaboration evidence | Good charter, some evidence | Basic | Little evidence |
| E-mail & research pack | Correct, complete steps; operators and CRAAP applied in a worked example; e-mail sent properly | Mostly complete | Errors | Not sent/incomplete |
| Virtual lesson | Secure, smooth, all features used, fallback demonstrated, URL/cookies explained | Smooth with minor issues | Basic meeting | Failed |
| Document & presentation | Professional, clear, within time | Clear | Basic | Weak |

