= Module 6 — Learning with Computers: E-services, E-learning and Simple Web Pages
**Weeks 12–13 · Lessons 42–46**
**Category of action:** Describing and using e-services (e-commerce, e-health, e-government); analysing applications, benefits and challenges of e-services; explaining computer-assisted learning and e-learning delivery; developing simple web pages with HTML.
**Context:** Learners examine the electronic services that increasingly mediate citizens' lives (42–43), understand how computers assist learning (44), integrate these ideas (45) and finish the term by building their own web pages (46).

# Lesson 42: E-services — E-commerce, E-health and E-government

> OBJ:
  - Define e-services and identify the main types (e-commerce, e-health, e-government, e-banking, e-learning…).
  - Describe how e-commerce, e-health and e-government services operate.
  - Use an e-service platform to perform a simple transaction or procedure (e.g., search a product, book an appointment, download a form).

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room/phones)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | "Which of these did your family do this month without visiting an office?" (pay a bill, buy online, check results, send money). | Raise hands; discuss. |
| Core 1 | 20 min | E-services: definition; characteristics (anytime/anywhere, self-service, automation, digital payment, data-driven); main types with Cameroonian and global examples: **e-commerce** (B2C, B2B, C2C, m-commerce, social commerce), **e-health**, **e-government** (G2C, G2B, G2G), e-banking/mobile money, e-learning (Lesson 44), e-ticketing/transport, e-agriculture, e-tourism, utilities (ENEO/Camwater bills). | Type–example matrix. |
| Core 2 | 35 min | How each operates: **e-commerce** (storefront/catalogue → cart → checkout → payment gateway (MoMo, Orange Money, card, cash on delivery) → order management → logistics/delivery → reviews; marketplaces vs own shops; WhatsApp/Facebook commerce; security – HTTPS, OTP, escrow), **e-health** (electronic health records, appointment booking, telemedicine, e-prescriptions, health information portals, DHIS2 reporting, mobile health SMS, lab results online, health insurance claims; privacy), **e-government** (portals, online forms, e-tax (impots.cm – DGI), e-visa, business registration (APME/CFCE), exam results (GCE Board, OBC), civil status/ID projects, e-procurement (COLEPS), digital payments of fees; identity/authentication; interoperability). | Process flowcharts. |
| Core 3 | 32 min | Hands-on: perform simple transactions/procedures (safe, no money): search and compare a product on Jumia (or a demo store), add to cart, read checkout options; book a demo appointment/locate a health facility on an e-health portal; download a form from an e-government site (e.g. DGI tax form, GCE registration guidelines, passport requirements) or check a results portal; note the URL, HTTPS, authentication and data requested. Step sheet + safety rules. | Complete tasks; record evidence. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 42.1 Definition and types of e-services

> DEF:
  **E-services (electronic services)** are **services delivered over electronic networks – mainly the internet and mobile networks – through websites, apps or USSD/SMS**, allowing users to obtain information, complete transactions or access public and private services without physical presence.

| Type | Meaning | Global examples | Cameroon examples |
| **E-commerce** | Buying/selling goods and services online: **B2C** (business to consumer), **B2B**, **C2C** (person to person), **m-commerce** (mobile), **social commerce** (via social apps) | Amazon, Alibaba, eBay, Shopify stores | **Jumia Cameroon**, Glotelho, Kikuu; WhatsApp/Facebook/Instagram shops; Yango/Gozem ride-hailing; food delivery apps |
| **E-health** | Health services and information via ICT: records, telemedicine, appointments, e-prescriptions, health data systems | NHS App (UK), Teladoc, Doctolib (France) | DHIS2 national health data; telemedicine pilots (Cardiopad by Arthur Zang), Gifted Mom / mHealth SMS for mothers, hospital appointment apps, health-insurance apps, COVID-19 vaccination platforms |
| **E-government** | Government services to citizens (**G2C**), businesses (**G2B**) and between agencies (**G2G**) online | Estonia e-Residency, gov.uk, India's Aadhaar/UMANG | **DGI impots.cm** (tax declaration/payment), e-visa (evisacam), GCE Board & OBC results portals, CONFEMEN/MINESEC portals, COLEPS e-procurement, business creation (CFCE), ENEO/Camwater e-billing, digital ID/civil status projects, MINFOPRA services |
| **E-banking & mobile money** | Banking and payments online/mobile | PayPal, Revolut, M-Pesa | **MTN MoMo**, Orange Money, bank apps (Afriland, UBA, Ecobank), Diool, Maviance/Smobilpay |
| **E-learning** | Courses and learning via ICT (Lesson 44) | Coursera, Khan Academy | MINESEC e-learning, university LMS |
| **E-ticketing / e-transport** | Tickets, bookings, ride-hailing | Booking.com, Uber | Bus companies' online booking, Camair-Co, Yango |
| **E-agriculture** | Market prices, advice, input ordering | Farmerline, iCow | Agrix Tech, cocoa price SMS services |
| **Utilities & telecoms self-service** | Bills, recharges, subscriptions | – | ENEO MyEasyLight, MTN/Orange apps and USSD |
| **E-tourism, e-recruitment, e-justice** | Reservations, jobs, case tracking | LinkedIn, Indeed | Njorku, MINTOUL portals |

Characteristics: 24/7 availability, self-service, automation, remote access, digital identity/authentication, electronic payment, data collection/analytics, multi-channel (web, app, USSD, SMS for feature phones), personalisation.

### 42.2 How e-commerce, e-health and e-government operate

| Stage of e-commerce | What happens | Technologies / actors |
| 1 Storefront & catalogue | Products with images, prices, stock, categories, search and filters | Web/app front-end, database, CMS (WooCommerce, Shopify), marketplace platform |
| 2 Account & cart | Customer registers/logs in; adds items | Authentication, session cookies |
| 3 Checkout | Delivery address, shipping method/cost, promo codes, order summary | Forms with validation (Lesson 25) |
| 4 **Payment** | Payment gateway processes MoMo/Orange Money (USSD push + PIN), cards (Visa/Mastercard with OTP/3-D Secure), PayPal, or **cash on delivery** (common in Cameroon due to trust) | Gateway APIs (Maviance, CinetPay, Flutterwave), HTTPS/TLS, PCI-DSS |
| 5 Order management | Confirmation e-mail/SMS, invoice, seller notified, stock updated | Back-office/ERP, SMTP/SMS APIs |
| 6 Fulfilment & logistics | Packing, courier/agent delivery, tracking, pick-up points | Logistics partners, GPS |
| 7 After-sales | Returns, refunds, reviews/ratings, customer support (chat/WhatsApp) | CRM, chatbots |
| Variants | Marketplace (many sellers, platform escrow) vs own shop; **social commerce** – catalogue on WhatsApp Business, payment by MoMo, delivery by moto-taxi | – |
| Security & trust | HTTPS, OTP, escrow/cash on delivery, verified sellers, reviews, data-protection law | – |

| E-health component | Operation | Example |
| Electronic health records (EHR/EMR) | Patient data stored centrally; accessible to authorised clinicians; access control and audit (Lesson 24) | OpenMRS in some hospitals |
| Appointment booking & queue | Online/app/USSD booking; SMS reminders | Hospital apps; Doctolib model |
| **Telemedicine/teleconsultation** | Video/phone consultation; remote diagnostics (Cardiopad transmits ECGs to cardiologists) | Rural clinics ↔ specialists |
| E-prescriptions & pharmacy | Digital prescriptions verified at pharmacies; stock systems | Pilot programmes |
| Health information & mHealth | Portals, chatbots, SMS campaigns (maternal health, vaccination) | Gifted Mom, MINSANTE WhatsApp lines |
| Health data systems | Facilities report weekly to **DHIS2**; dashboards for epidemics | National HMIS |
| Lab/imaging results online | Patient portals; PACS for images | Hospitals in Douala/Yaoundé |
| Insurance & payments | Claims, mobile money for fees | Insurance apps |
| Constraints | Privacy/consent, connectivity, digital literacy, interoperability, power | – |

| E-government component | Operation | Example |
| Information portals | Laws, procedures, forms, announcements | Ministry sites (.gov.cm), spm.gov.cm |
| Online forms & downloads | PDF/online forms to prepare files | DGI forms, passport requirements, GCE registration |
| Transactional services | Declare/pay taxes online, apply for e-visa, register a business, check results | **impots.cm**, evisacam.cm, CFCE, GCE Board results |
| Identity & authentication | Taxpayer number (NIU), passwords/OTP, digital ID projects | DGI account, e-visa reference |
| Payments | Mobile money/bank integration | Tax and fee payments via MoMo |
| Back-office & interoperability | Databases, workflow, G2G data exchange | Civil status, customs (CAMCIS), e-procurement (COLEPS) |
| Benefits/challenges | Convenience, transparency, reduced corruption vs digital divide, trust, uptime, data protection (Law 2024/017) | – |

### 42.3 Using an e-service platform (hands-on)

**Safety rules:** use only HTTPS official domains (check the URL as in Lesson 40); no real payments or personal IDs in class; never enter card/MoMo PINs on a shared PC; use demo accounts where needed; log out.

| Task (choose two or three) | Steps | Evidence to record |
| **E-commerce – search and compare a product** | 1. Open jumia.cm (or a demo store). 2. Search "laptop 8GB". 3. Filter by price and rating; sort. 4. Open two products; compare specs, seller rating, delivery fee/time, return policy. 5. Add one to cart; view cart; proceed to checkout **up to** the payment-method page (stop). 6. Note payment options (MoMo, card, cash on delivery). | Screenshots; comparison table; URL; HTTPS; data requested |
| **E-health – locate a facility or book a (demo) appointment** | 1. Open a health portal/app (MINSANTE site, a hospital site, or a demo booking form). 2. Find the nearest facility and its services/hours; 3. Fill a demo appointment form (fictitious data) noting validation rules; 4. Read the privacy notice. | Facility info; form fields; privacy notes |
| **E-government – download a form / check a procedure** | 1. Open impots.cm ▸ Forms, or the GCE Board site, or the passport service page. 2. Locate the required form/guide (e.g. annual declaration form, GCE registration timetable, passport requirements list). 3. Download the PDF; verify its source. 4. List the documents and steps required; 5. Note authentication needed for transactional steps. | PDF saved; step list; URL check |
| **Mobile money / utility (simulation)** | Use the USSD menu tree printed by the teacher (*126#, #150#) to simulate paying an ENEO bill – steps only. | Menu path; security steps (PIN) |

> EXAMPLE:
  **Paul's Saturday of e-services in Yaoundé.** 08:00 – pays the ENEO bill via MoMo USSD (utility e-service); 09:00 – orders a phone charger on Jumia, chooses cash on delivery (e-commerce); 10:00 – books his mother's follow-up appointment on the hospital app and receives an SMS reminder (e-health); 11:00 – downloads his father's tax declaration form from impots.cm and later watches him file it online (e-government); 12:00 – checks Concours results on the ministry portal. Not one queue – but each step required a phone, data, literacy and trust; his grandmother in the village would have needed help with all five.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 42.1 – E-services map** with icons for each type and Cameroonian logos/platforms. **Figure 42.2 – E-commerce order flowchart** (browse → cart → checkout → payment gateway → confirmation → delivery → review) with security checkpoints. **Figure 42.3 – E-health ecosystem** (patient app, EHR, telemedicine, pharmacy, DHIS2 reporting). **Figure 42.4 – E-government service model** (citizen/business → portal → authentication → back-office systems → payment → outcome) and a screenshot mock-up of a form-download page with the URL highlighted.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "E-commerce means paying online." Cash on delivery and MoMo on delivery are e-commerce too – the transaction is initiated online. "E-government is just a website with information." Full e-government is transactional (declare, pay, register, receive documents).

> ACTIVITY:
  **E-service explorers (50 min).** (1) Type–example matrix completed with two local examples per type. (2) Hands-on tasks from the table above (two or three), with evidence sheets. (3) Process mapping: draw the e-commerce flowchart for a WhatsApp-based shop vs Jumia; draw the e-visa or tax-declaration flow. (4) Accessibility check: how would a user with a feature phone or no literacy access each service (USSD, agents, kiosks)? (5) No-computer alternative: printed screenshots/USSD trees; paper walkthrough of an order and a form download.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Define e-services and list six types with one Cameroonian example each.
  2. Distinguish B2C, B2B and C2C e-commerce with examples.
  3. Describe the stages of an online purchase from catalogue to delivery, naming the technologies involved at payment.
  4. Describe three e-health services and three e-government services and how they operate.
  5. Outline the steps to download a form from an e-government portal safely, and to compare two products on an e-commerce site.
  6. Why is cash on delivery common in Cameroonian e-commerce, and what security features build trust online?
  **Homework:** With a family member, carry out (or observe) one real e-service task (bill payment, results check, form download, online purchase) and write a one-page account: steps, channel (web/app/USSD), authentication, payment, problems and what would help first-time users.

