# Lesson 43: Applications, Benefits and Challenges of E-services

> OBJ:
  - Analyse the applications of e-services in different sectors (education, health, finance, administration, commerce).
  - Evaluate the benefits and challenges of e-services for citizens, businesses and government.
  - Explore e-government platforms and assess their usability and accessibility.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Story: a farmer in Ndop receiving cocoa prices by SMS vs a graduate stuck because a portal is down on the deadline day. | React. |
| Core 1 | 25 min | Sector analysis: **education** (registration/results portals, LMS, digital libraries, scholarship applications, school fees via MoMo), **health** (records, telemedicine, vaccination campaigns, drug authentication (mPedigree-style), insurance), **finance** (mobile money, online banking, micro-loans/credit scoring, insurance, remittances, crowdfunding), **administration** (tax, customs CAMCIS, civil status, procurement, licences, e-visa, public-sector HR SIGIPES), **commerce** (marketplaces, social commerce, logistics, payments, advertising); for each: applications, users, enabling technologies, indicators of adoption. | Sector cards. |
| Core 2 | 30 min | Benefits vs challenges matrix per stakeholder – **citizens** (convenience, time/cost savings, transparency, inclusion of remote areas vs digital divide, literacy, cost of data/devices, trust, privacy, fraud), **businesses** (market reach, lower costs, efficiency, data, formalisation vs competition, cybersecurity, logistics, payment fraud, regulation, skills), **government** (efficiency, revenue, reduced corruption, better data, service quality vs investment, legacy systems, interoperability, capacity, resistance to change, security, exclusion risks, sustainability/power). Cameroonian evidence: mobile-money penetration, ANTIC cyber-incident reports, 2024 data-protection law, connectivity gaps; global frameworks (UN E-Government Development Index – Cameroon's rank; SDGs). | Stakeholder evaluation. |
| Core 3 | 32 min | Explore e-government platforms hands-on: at least two of impots.cm, evisacam.cm, GCE Board results, CFCE business creation, MINESEC/MINESUP portals, a council site; **usability heuristics** checklist (Nielsen-inspired: clarity, navigation, consistency, feedback, error prevention/recovery, efficiency, help), **accessibility** checklist (WCAG basics: text alternatives, contrast, keyboard navigation, font resizing, plain language, French/English, mobile friendliness, low-bandwidth, offline/USSD alternatives), performance (load time, HTTPS, uptime), trust signals (contact, privacy policy, official domain). Groups audit and score; propose improvements. | Platform audits. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 43.1 Applications of e-services by sector

| Sector | Applications | Users | Enabling technologies | Cameroon examples |
| **Education** | Online registration and results (GCE, Concours, BEPC/Probatoire/Bac), LMS and e-learning, digital libraries/OER, scholarship and university applications, fee payment via mobile money, school management systems, timetables/report cards, virtual classes | Learners, parents, teachers, ministries | Web portals, LMS (Moodle, Classroom), payment APIs, SMS | GCE Board results portal, OBC/MINESEC results, university online pre-registration (UY1, UB), fee payment via MoMo/Express Union, MINESEC e-learning platform |
| **Health** | Electronic records, appointment systems, telemedicine, e-prescriptions, health information/chatbots, vaccination and epidemic dashboards, drug-authentication SMS, insurance claims, blood-bank locators | Patients, clinicians, MINSANTE, insurers | EHR software, video, DHIS2, SMS/USSD, IoT (Cardiopad) | DHIS2, Cardiopad, Gifted Mom, COVID-19 vaccination portal, hospital apps |
| **Finance** | Mobile money (transfers, bill pay, merchant pay, savings), online/mobile banking, micro-loans with digital credit scoring, insurance, remittances, crowdfunding, stock/forex apps, payroll | Individuals, SMEs, banks, MFIs | USSD/apps, APIs, KYC systems, AI scoring | MTN MoMo, Orange Money, Afriland/UBA apps, Diool, Maviance, WorldRemit/Wave inflows |
| **Administration (e-government)** | Tax declaration/payment, customs (CAMCIS), business registration, e-visa/passports, civil status/ID, e-procurement (COLEPS), public-service HR/payroll (SIGIPES), land registry pilots, licences/permits, council services, open data | Citizens, businesses, civil servants | Portals, digital identity, interoperability platforms, payment gateways | DGI impots.cm, CAMCIS, evisacam, CFCE, COLEPS, SIGIPES, MINFOPRA |
| **Commerce** | Marketplaces, own web shops, social commerce, classified ads, logistics/tracking, digital marketing, loyalty, B2B procurement, ride-hailing and delivery | Consumers, traders, SMEs, logistics firms | E-commerce platforms, payment gateways, GPS, social media APIs | Jumia, Glotelho, Kikuu, WhatsApp shops, Yango/Gozem, Jumia Food |
| Agriculture, energy, transport, tourism | Price information, extension advice, input e-vouchers; smart metering; ticketing; bookings | Farmers, utilities, travellers | SMS/USSD, IoT, apps | Agrix Tech, ENEO MyEasyLight, bus e-tickets |

### 43.2 Benefits and challenges by stakeholder

| Stakeholder | Benefits | Challenges |
| **Citizens** | Convenience 24/7 and from anywhere (no travel from Kumbo to Yaoundé for a form); time and cost savings; **transparency** (published fees, tracking, fewer intermediaries and bribes); access for remote/disabled users; faster services (results in minutes); financial inclusion via mobile money; information and choice | **Digital divide** (rural coverage, data/device cost, electricity); digital and language literacy; **trust** (fear of fraud, unclear procedures); **privacy and data protection**; fraud/phishing and impersonation; system downtime on deadline days; lack of human help; exclusion of the elderly/informal sector; dependence on phones/SIM registration |
| **Businesses** | Wider markets (national/diaspora/export); lower transaction and marketing costs; efficiency and automation; payment traceability; data on customers; formalisation and access to credit; faster government procedures (registration in days) | Competition from global platforms; **cybersecurity** (fraud, chargebacks, hacked accounts); logistics and addressing (no street numbers); payment failures/fees; regulatory compliance (taxes, data law); skills shortage; connectivity costs; counterfeit and trust issues on marketplaces |
| **Government** | Efficiency and cost reduction; **increased revenue** (tax base widening, less leakage); reduced corruption; better data for planning (DHIS2, tax analytics); improved service quality/image; interoperability between agencies; crisis resilience (COVID-19) | High investment and maintenance; legacy systems and paper archives; **interoperability** and data standards; skills and capacity; **resistance to change** and vested interests; cybersecurity and sovereignty (hosting, backups); uptime/power; risk of excluding citizens; legal frameworks (e-signature, data protection – Law 2024/017); sustainability of donor-funded platforms |

Evaluation lens: measure benefits (time saved, cost, uptake %, revenue, satisfaction) against challenges; propose mitigations – multi-channel access (USSD, agents/kiosks, community centres), local-language interfaces, offline modes, strong authentication with simple UX, awareness campaigns, help desks, open standards/APIs, cybersecurity investment, data-protection compliance, phased rollout with feedback.

### 43.3 Exploring and assessing e-government platforms

> KEY:
  **Usability** – how easily and efficiently users achieve their goals; **accessibility** – whether people with disabilities, low literacy, old devices or weak connections can use the service at all. Good e-government must be usable, accessible, secure and trustworthy.

| Dimension | Checklist questions (score 0–2 each) |
| **Findability & navigation** | Is the service easy to find from the home page/Google? Clear menus, breadcrumbs, search? |
| **Clarity & language** | Plain language? Both French and English? Steps and required documents listed? Fees stated? |
| **Consistency & design** | Consistent layout, official branding, readable fonts, no clutter? |
| **Feedback & status** | Confirmations, progress indicators, reference numbers, SMS/e-mail notifications? |
| **Error prevention & recovery** | Validation with helpful messages (Lesson 25)? Can users correct mistakes? Save and resume? |
| **Efficiency** | Number of steps/clicks; pre-filled data; no repeated data entry; mobile-friendly forms |
| **Help & support** | FAQs, guides/videos, contact (phone/e-mail/chat), response quality |
| **Accessibility (WCAG basics)** | Alt text for images, sufficient contrast, keyboard navigation, text resizing, captions, simple structure/headings, screen-reader compatibility |
| **Inclusion & channels** | Works on low-end phones/2G? USSD/SMS or agent alternative? Offline forms? Local languages/audio? |
| **Performance & availability** | Load time on 3G; uptime (test at different times); page weight |
| **Security & trust** | HTTPS and correct official domain; privacy policy; secure login/OTP; contact details; data-protection compliance; no suspicious ads |
| **Overall value** | Does it complete the transaction end-to-end (apply → pay → receive)? or only information? |

**Audit procedure:** choose a platform and a realistic task (e.g. "find and download the tax declaration form", "check O-Level results for a given centre", "start an e-visa application to the payment step"); time the task; count steps; note obstacles; run a mobile view (F12 ▸ device toolbar) and a contrast/alt-text check (browser accessibility tools, WAVE extension); score with the checklist; write three prioritised recommendations.

> EXAMPLE:
  **Two portals, two experiences.** Auditing the GCE results portal: task completed in 3 steps and 40 s on a phone, HTTPS official domain, but tiny fonts, no alt text and downtime at peak – usability high, accessibility medium, availability low; recommendation: CDN/caching and SMS results channel. Auditing a business-registration portal: bilingual, clear document list, OTP login, but a 12-step form without save/resume and error messages in one language only; a diaspora entrepreneur in Belgium abandons at step 9 – recommendation: save-and-resume, bilingual validation messages, progress bar. Both audits show the difference between publishing information online and delivering a usable, inclusive service.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 43.1 – Sector wheel of e-service applications** with Cameroonian examples per sector. **Figure 43.2 – Stakeholder benefit/challenge matrix** as a 3×2 grid with icons. **Figure 43.3 – E-government maturity ladder**: information → interaction (forms) → transaction (pay/apply) → integration (joined-up, proactive services); place local platforms on the ladder. **Figure 43.4 – Usability & accessibility scorecard** mock-up with radar chart for an audited portal, plus mobile vs desktop screenshots.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "If a service is online, everyone can use it." Inclusion requires channels for those without smartphones, data, literacy or sight. "Usability is cosmetic." Abandoned applications, wrong declarations and help-desk overload are costly consequences of poor usability.

> ACTIVITY:
  **E-government audit (55 min, groups of three).** (1) Sector cards: each group prepares a 90-second brief on one sector's applications with a local example and an adoption indicator. (2) Stakeholder debate: groups argue benefits/challenges from the viewpoint of a rural citizen, an SME owner and a ministry CIO; record mitigations. (3) Platform audit: complete the checklist and timed task on two platforms (one national, one council/ministry); produce a scorecard and three recommendations; present. (4) Redesign sketch: paper wireframe of one improved form page (labels, validation messages, progress bar, language toggle, mobile layout). (5) No-computer alternative: printed screenshots of portals for audit; paper wireframes.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Analyse the applications of e-services in education, health, finance, administration and commerce, giving two examples per sector.
  2. Evaluate three benefits and three challenges of e-services for citizens, businesses and government respectively.
  3. Propose four measures to reduce the exclusion caused by the digital divide in e-government.
  4. Distinguish usability from accessibility and list five criteria for each.
  5. Describe how you would audit an e-government platform and what evidence you would collect.
  6. Place three Cameroonian platforms on the e-government maturity ladder and justify.
  **Homework:** Audit one e-government or e-service platform of your choice with the checklist (timed task, scores, screenshots), and write a one-page memo to its managers with three prioritised, justified improvements.

