# Lesson 44: Computer-Assisted Learning and E-learning

> OBJ:
  - Define computer-assisted learning (CAL) and e-learning and outline their development.
  - Describe the delivery of e-learning through servers (LMS, web platforms), CD-ROMs/DVDs and e-books.
  - Evaluate the advantages and limitations of e-learning compared with traditional learning.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | How did you last learn something new outside class – YouTube, WhatsApp group, app, book? | Share. |
| Core 1 | 20 min | Definitions: **CAL/CAI** (computer used as a tutor/tool/drill – tutorials, drill-and-practice, simulations, games, problem solving), **e-learning** (learning delivered through digital technologies and networks: online courses, LMS, MOOCs, blended/hybrid, mobile learning, microlearning), related terms (CBT, distance learning, virtual classroom, flipped classroom, adaptive learning, AI tutors); development: 1960s PLATO, 1980s CD-ROM courseware, 1990s web, 2000s LMS (Moodle 2002)/Web 2.0, 2008–12 MOOCs (Coursera, edX, Khan Academy), 2010s mobile apps, 2020 pandemic shift, 2023+ generative-AI tutors; Cameroon: distance-learning programmes, MINESEC/MINESUP platforms, university e-learning centres. | Concept map; timeline. |
| Core 2 | 35 min | Delivery modes: **servers/online** – LMS (Moodle, Google Classroom, Teams for Education, Canvas), MOOC platforms, video (YouTube), web apps, live virtual classes, mobile apps – architecture (client–server, cloud), features (content, quizzes, forums, gradebook, tracking, SCORM/xAPI), requirements (connectivity, accounts); **CD-ROM/DVD & offline media** – packaged courseware, encyclopaedias (Encarta), language courses, offline copies of Wikipedia/Khan Academy (Kiwix, RACHEL servers, Raspberry Pi content servers) – valuable where internet is weak; **e-books** – formats (PDF, EPUB, MOBI), readers (Kindle, phones, tablets), interactive e-books, OER textbooks, digital libraries (Project Gutenberg, OpenStax, African Storybook), audio-books, accessibility (text-to-speech). Hands-on: explore a Moodle/Classroom demo course; open an EPUB; browse an offline Kiwix library. | Explore platforms; compare delivery. |
| Core 3 | 32 min | Evaluation vs traditional learning: advantages (flexibility, access, cost at scale, self-pacing, rich media/simulations, tracking/analytics, personalisation, updating content, inclusion of remote learners), limitations (connectivity/device/power, self-discipline and dropout, reduced social interaction and mentoring, practical/lab skills, assessment integrity, teacher training, quality control, screen fatigue, cost of production); comparison table; blended learning as synthesis; criteria for evaluating an e-learning resource; case studies (2020 closures in Cameroon; a rural school with a Raspberry Pi content server). | Comparison; resource evaluation. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 44.1 Definitions and development

> DEF:
  **Computer-assisted learning (CAL)** – also computer-assisted/aided instruction (CAI) – is the **use of computers to support teaching and learning**, with software acting as tutor (tutorials, drill and practice), tool (simulations, modelling, word processing) or tutee (learners programming the computer). **E-learning (electronic learning)** is **learning delivered and supported through digital technologies and networks** – online courses, learning management systems, virtual classrooms, mobile apps and digital content – whether fully online, blended with face-to-face teaching, or offline on digital media.

| Term | Meaning |
| Tutorial software | Presents content step by step with checks (e.g. typing tutors, Khan Academy lessons) |
| Drill and practice | Repetitive exercises with feedback (maths facts, vocabulary apps like Duolingo) |
| Simulation & educational games | Safe experimentation (PhET physics sims, virtual chemistry labs, flight simulators, Minecraft Education) |
| Computer-based training (CBT) | Self-paced training modules, often corporate/vocational |
| Distance learning | Learners and teachers separated in space/time (correspondence → radio/TV → online) |
| **LMS (Learning Management System)** | Platform hosting courses, materials, activities, quizzes, grades (Moodle, Canvas, Google Classroom, Teams) |
| **MOOC** | Massive Open Online Course (Coursera, edX, FutureLearn, OpenClassrooms) |
| Blended/hybrid learning; flipped classroom | Mix of online and in-person; content at home, practice in class |
| Mobile learning (m-learning), microlearning | Learning via phones in short units (apps, WhatsApp lessons, SMS quizzes) |
| Adaptive learning & AI tutors | Software adjusting difficulty/paths to the learner; chat-based tutors (Module 1) |
| Virtual classroom | Live online lessons with video, whiteboard, chat (Lesson 40) |

| Period | Development |
| 1960s–70s | PLATO system (University of Illinois) – first large CAI network; Skinner's teaching machines; Logo (Papert) |
| 1980s | Personal computers in schools; drill software; BBC Micro; early authoring tools |
| 1990s | Multimedia **CD-ROM** courseware and encyclopaedias (Encarta), the Web, first online courses |
| 2000s | LMS era (Blackboard, **Moodle 2002**), SCORM standards, Web 2.0 (wikis, YouTube 2005), Khan Academy 2008 |
| 2011–2015 | **MOOCs** (Stanford AI course, Coursera, edX), tablets, e-books, flipped classrooms, Google Classroom (2014) |
| 2016–2019 | Mobile-first learning, gamification, analytics, OER growth, African EdTech (Eneza, uLesson) |
| 2020–2022 | COVID-19 school closures – emergency remote teaching (radio/TV/WhatsApp/Zoom); Cameroon's MINESEC distance-learning programmes on CRTV and online |
| 2023– | Generative-AI tutors and content creation, adaptive platforms, micro-credentials, offline-first content servers for low-connectivity areas |

### 44.2 Delivery of e-learning

| Delivery channel | How it works | Examples | Requirements | Strengths | Limits |
| **Servers – LMS/web platforms (online)** | Content and activities hosted on a web server/cloud; learners log in through browsers/apps; teacher creates courses, uploads resources, sets quizzes/assignments/forums; system tracks progress and grades; standards (SCORM, xAPI, LTI) allow content interchange | **Moodle** (open source, school-hostable), **Google Classroom**, **Microsoft Teams for Education**, Canvas, Schoology; MOOC platforms; YouTube channels; virtual classes on Meet/Zoom; mobile apps (Duolingo, uLesson); MINESEC/MINESUP e-learning portals | Internet connectivity, devices, accounts, server/cloud hosting, teacher digital skills | Anywhere/anytime, always updated, interaction (forums, live classes), analytics, scalable | Depends on connectivity/power/data cost; digital divide; hosting costs; needs moderation |
| **Offline media – CD-ROM/DVD/USB and local content servers** | Courseware, videos and encyclopaedias distributed physically or preloaded; run on PCs without internet; modern equivalent: **offline content servers** (Kiwix offline Wikipedia, RACHEL, Kolibri/Khan Academy Lite on a Raspberry Pi serving the school Wi-Fi) | Encarta, language CDs, exam-revision DVDs, textbook companion discs; Kiwix; Kolibri; USB packs of MINESEC videos | Computer with drive/USB or a local server + LAN/Wi-Fi (no internet) | Works without internet, no data costs, fast, controlled content | Content can become outdated; no live interaction; physical distribution; drives now rare on laptops |
| **E-books and digital texts** | Digital books in **PDF**, **EPUB** (reflowable), MOBI/AZW (Kindle); read on phones, tablets, e-readers, PCs; may include search, highlights, notes, dictionary, text-to-speech, embedded video/quizzes (interactive e-books); distributed via stores, libraries or OER repositories | OpenStax textbooks, Project Gutenberg, African Storybook, Bookshare (accessible), school PDF notes, Kindle/Google Play Books, Cameroonian publishers' digital textbooks | Reader app/device; storage; (download once – offline afterwards) | Portable library, searchable, cheap/free OER, accessibility features, easy to update | Screen reading fatigue, DRM restrictions, piracy, device dependence, no interaction unless interactive |
| Broadcast & messaging (complementary) | TV/radio lessons, WhatsApp/Telegram groups, SMS quizzes | CRTV school programmes (2020), WhatsApp revision groups | Basic phone/TV | Reach without internet/smartphones | One-way; limited feedback |

**Typical blended set-up for a Cameroonian school:** Google Classroom or a school-hosted Moodle for assignments and quizzes; a Raspberry Pi/PC **offline server** with Kiwix and Khan Academy videos on the lab Wi-Fi; PDF/EPUB notes on phones; WhatsApp class group for reminders; occasional Meet sessions with guest experts; printed packs for learners without devices.

### 44.3 Evaluating e-learning against traditional learning

| Criterion | E-learning | Traditional (face-to-face) |
| Flexibility of time/place | Anytime, anywhere; self-paced | Fixed timetable and location |
| Access & scale | Reaches remote learners and thousands at once; MOOCs from top universities | Limited by classrooms and teachers; travel |
| Cost | Low per learner at scale; high initial content/platform cost; learner bears data/device costs | Buildings, staff, travel; low technology cost |
| Interaction & mentoring | Forums, chat, video; can feel isolated; less spontaneous | Rich human interaction, immediate feedback, role models, peer learning |
| Personalisation | Adaptive paths, repeat videos, analytics, AI tutors | Depends on class size; teacher's intuition |
| Content richness & currency | Multimedia, simulations, instantly updated | Textbooks age; practicals real |
| Practical/hands-on skills | Simulations partial; labs, sports, arts hard | Direct practice |
| Discipline & completion | Requires self-motivation; MOOC completion often < 10 % | Structure and supervision |
| Assessment | Auto-graded quizzes, analytics; integrity challenges (proctoring) | Supervised exams; slower feedback |
| Inclusion | Helps some (mobility, remote) – excludes those without devices/connectivity/literacy | Excludes those far away or with mobility issues |
| Teacher role | Designer, facilitator, needs digital skills | Instructor, mentor |
| Social & emotional development | Limited | Strong (school life) |
| Resilience | Continues during crises (pandemic, strikes) | Interrupted |
| **Best of both** | **Blended learning** – online content and practice, in-person discussion, labs and support | – |

Criteria for evaluating an e-learning resource: learning objectives clear and aligned; accuracy and authority; interactivity and feedback; accessibility (language, captions, low bandwidth, offline option); usability; assessment quality; cost/licence (OER preferred); cultural relevance (Cameroonian syllabus/examples); data privacy for minors; evidence of effectiveness.

> EXAMPLE:
  **Two schools in 2020 and after.** During closures, School A relied on Zoom lessons – 30 % of learners joined (data, devices, power). School B distributed PDF notes and WhatsApp voice explanations, ran CRTV-synchronised revision and, on reopening, installed a 60,000 XAF Raspberry Pi with Kiwix, Khan Academy Lite and the teachers' videos on the lab Wi-Fi; learners now revise from phones at school without internet, submit quizzes on a Moodle hosted on the same Pi, and use Google Classroom at home when they have data. School B's blended, offline-first design shows how delivery via servers, offline media and e-books can be combined to fit local realities.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 44.1 – CAL/e-learning concept map** (tutorial, drill, simulation, LMS, MOOC, blended, mobile, AI tutor) with a development timeline. **Figure 44.2 – Delivery architectures**: (a) cloud LMS ↔ internet ↔ learners; (b) offline content server on school LAN/Wi-Fi; (c) e-book flow from OER repository to devices – with requirement icons (internet, power, device). **Figure 44.3 – Comparison chart** e-learning vs traditional as a balanced scale leading to a "blended" centre. **Figure 44.4 – Screenshot mock-ups** of a Moodle course page, a Kiwix library and an EPUB reader with highlights/text-to-speech.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "E-learning means watching videos." Effective e-learning includes practice, feedback, interaction and assessment. "E-learning needs internet." Offline servers, CD/USB content and downloaded e-books deliver e-learning without connectivity.

> ACTIVITY:
  **E-learning explorers (55 min).** (1) Platform tour: log into a demo Moodle/Google Classroom course – submit an assignment, take a quiz, post in a forum; list the LMS features observed. (2) Offline media: browse Kiwix (offline Wikipedia) or a Khan Academy Lite/Kolibri server on the lab network; compare speed and content freshness with the online version. (3) E-books: open the same text as PDF and EPUB on a phone/PC; test reflow, search, highlight, text-to-speech; note accessibility differences. (4) Evaluation: score two e-learning resources (one local, one MOOC) with the criteria list; decide which suits a Form 3 learner in a rural school and why. (5) Design task: propose a blended plan for one subject in your school (online, offline, e-book, printed) with a budget under 100,000 XAF. (6) No-computer alternative: sample screenshots and printed course pages; role-play an LMS workflow with paper "submissions".

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Define CAL and e-learning and distinguish tutorial, drill-and-practice and simulation software.
  2. Outline five milestones in the development of e-learning.
  3. Describe e-learning delivery via servers/LMS, via CD-ROM/offline media and via e-books, with requirements, strengths and limits of each.
  4. What is an LMS? Name three and five features.
  5. Evaluate e-learning against traditional learning on six criteria and explain why blended learning is often recommended.
  6. List six criteria for evaluating an e-learning resource for Cameroonian learners.
  **Homework:** Review one e-learning resource you can access (app, YouTube channel, MOOC, e-book, offline pack) with the criteria list; write a 250-word review and a recommendation to your school's ICT committee on how it could be delivered (online, offline server, e-book).

---

# Lesson 45: Integration Activities — Designing a Digital Services and Learning Programme

> ACTIVITY:
  **Context.** The Parent–Teacher Association of your school wants to (a) move fee payment, results consultation and enrolment online, (b) partner with the district hospital for a student e-health card and vaccination reminders, (c) help parents use e-government services, and (d) launch a blended e-learning programme that works with unreliable internet. They need a feasibility study and a demonstrator.
  **Task (groups of 4, 2 periods).**
  1. **E-services design** (Lesson 42): describe how the school's e-services would operate – enrolment form → validation → fee payment via MoMo/Orange Money → receipt → results portal; draw the process flowchart with authentication and payment gateway; specify channels (web, USSD/SMS, agent desk) for parents without smartphones; identify the e-health integration (appointment/reminder flow, consent and data protection) and two e-government services parents most need (tax form, civil status/ID, exam registration) with step-by-step guides.
  2. **Benefits, challenges and usability** (Lesson 43): a stakeholder benefit/challenge matrix (parents, school, hospital, ministry) with mitigations; a usability/accessibility audit of one existing platform the school already depends on (results portal or a bank/MoMo app) using the checklist, with a scorecard and improvements; a bilingual, mobile-first wireframe (paper or Canva/Figma) of the enrolment-and-payment page applying validation messages, progress bar and accessibility rules.
  3. **E-learning delivery plan** (Lesson 44): choose and justify the delivery mix – cloud LMS (Google Classroom/Moodle), offline content server (Raspberry Pi with Kiwix/Kolibri) and e-books (PDF/EPUB OER), plus WhatsApp/printed fallbacks; budget under 500,000 XAF; teacher-training outline; evaluation criteria for the resources; comparison with the current traditional approach and expected benefits/limitations.
  4. **Demonstrator**: (a) a working demo enrolment form with validation (Google Forms/Sheets or a spreadsheet with Data Validation) and a simulated payment confirmation e-mail; (b) a demo course in Classroom/Moodle with one resource, one quiz and one assignment; (c) if possible, a Kiwix/Kolibri page served on the lab network or an EPUB opened on a phone; (d) a short screen recording or live walk-through.
  5. **Deliver** a 6–8 page feasibility report (PDF, produced collaboratively with version history) and an 8-minute presentation with the demonstrator; e-mail the report to the teacher with proper etiquette.
  **Criteria.** Correct and complete e-service process design with inclusive channels and data-protection awareness; rigorous stakeholder analysis and evidence-based platform audit; realistic, costed e-learning delivery plan that fits local constraints; working demonstrator; quality of report, wireframe and presentation; collaboration and communication evidence.

## Rubric

| Criterion | 4 – Excellent | 3 – Good | 2 – Developing | 1 – Beginning |
| E-services process design | Complete flows (enrolment, payment, results, e-health, e-gov guides), inclusive channels, security/consent addressed | Complete with minor gaps | Partial | Incoherent |
| Stakeholder analysis & audit | Rich matrix with mitigations; audit with scores, evidence and prioritised fixes; strong wireframe | Good analysis, adequate audit | Superficial | Missing |
| E-learning delivery plan | Justified mix, costed, training and evaluation criteria, honest limitations | Justified, less detailed | Generic | Unrealistic |
| Demonstrator | Form with validation, confirmation e-mail, demo course, offline/e-book element working | Most elements work | One element | None |
| Report, presentation & collaboration | Professional, bilingual-aware, within time, clear collaboration evidence, proper e-mail | Clear | Basic | Weak |

