# Lesson 46: Developing Simple Web Pages with HTML

> OBJ:
  - Describe the structure of an HTML document and the role of tags and attributes.
  - Use basic HTML tags (headings, paragraphs, lists, links, images, tables) and attributes to create content.
  - Create and display simple web pages using a text editor and a web browser.

## Lesson outline (3 periods, 150 min – computer room)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 6 min | Open any web page ▸ right-click ▸ View page source; "This is what the browser reads." Recall URLs and HTTP (Lessons 32, 40). | Observe source. |
| Core 1 | 30 min | HTML basics: what HTML is (HyperText Markup Language, W3C/WHATWG standard, HTML5); document structure (`<!DOCTYPE html>`, `<html lang>`, `<head>` with `<meta charset>`, `<title>`; `<body>`); **elements, tags** (opening/closing, void elements), **attributes** (name="value" – `href`, `src`, `alt`, `width`, `id`, `class`, `lang`, `target`), nesting and indentation, comments, case, whitespace; separation from CSS (style) and JavaScript (behaviour) – mention only. Live-code a first page in Notepad/Notepad++/VS Code/gedit; save as `.html`; open in browser; edit–save–refresh cycle. | Create first page. |
| Core 2 | 60 min | Basic tags with attributes: headings `<h1>`–`<h6>`, paragraphs `<p>`, line break `<br>`, horizontal rule `<hr>`, emphasis `<strong>`, `<em>`, lists `<ul>`, `<ol>`, `<li>` (with `type`/`start`), links `<a href target>` (absolute, relative, e-mail, anchors), images `<img src alt width height>`, tables `<table>`, `<tr>`, `<th>`, `<td>` (`border`, `colspan`, `rowspan`, `<caption>`), semantic containers `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<footer>`, `<div>`, `<span>`; simple inline `style` attribute and a `<style>` block for colour/font (light touch); `<meta name="viewport">` for mobile. Worked example built progressively: "My School" page. | Follow along; type and test. |
| Core 3 | 44 min | Hands-on project: two-page mini-site (home + about) linked to each other, with an image folder, a table, lists, an external link and an e-mail link; validate at validator.w3.org; view on a phone via the lab share or by opening the file. Debugging common errors (unclosed tags, wrong path, missing quotes). | Build, test, validate. |
| Conclusion | 10 min | Gallery walk; exit ticket; preview of Second Term topics (not covered here). | Show pages; answer. |

## Core content

### 46.1 HTML, tags and attributes

> DEF:
  **HTML (HyperText Markup Language)** is the standard language for **structuring content on web pages**. An HTML document is plain text containing **elements** marked up with **tags** – keywords in angle brackets such as `<p>` … `</p>` – that tell the browser what each piece of content is (heading, paragraph, link, image, table). **Attributes** are name–value pairs inside an opening tag (e.g. `<img src="logo.png" alt="School logo">`) that provide extra information or settings for the element.

| Concept | Explanation | Example |
| **Element** | Opening tag + content + closing tag | `<h1>Welcome</h1>` |
| **Tag** | Markup keyword in `< >`; closing tag has `/` | `<p>` … `</p>` |
| **Void (empty) element** | No content or closing tag | `<br>`, `<hr>`, `<img>`, `<meta>`, `<link>` |
| **Attribute** | `name="value"` in the opening tag; several allowed; lower-case; quotes | `<a href="https://www.gceboard.cm" target="_blank">` |
| **Nesting** | Elements inside elements, properly closed in reverse order | `<p>This is <strong>important</strong>.</p>` |
| **Comment** | Ignored by browser | `<!-- navigation starts here -->` |
| **Whitespace** | Multiple spaces/newlines collapse to one space | Use `<br>` or `<p>` for breaks |
| **Case & extension** | Tags lower-case by convention; files `.html`; `index.html` = default home page | – |
| Global attributes | Usable on most elements | `id`, `class`, `style`, `title`, `lang` |

**Document structure:**

```
<!DOCTYPE html>                       <!-- tells the browser this is HTML5 -->
<html lang="en">                      <!-- root element; language of the page -->
  <head>                              <!-- information ABOUT the page (not displayed) -->
    <meta charset="UTF-8">            <!-- character encoding (accents, symbols) -->
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>My School – Home</title>   <!-- shown in the tab/bookmarks/search results -->
  </head>
  <body>                              <!-- everything displayed -->
    <h1>Welcome to GBHS Etoug-Ebe</h1>
    <p>Founded in 1979, our school serves over 3,000 learners.</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

### 46.2 Basic tags and attributes

| Purpose | Tags | Key attributes | Example |
| Headings | `<h1>` (most important, one per page) to `<h6>` | `id` (anchor target) | `<h2 id="contact">Contact us</h2>` |
| Paragraphs & text | `<p>`, `<br>` (line break), `<hr>` (thematic break), `<strong>` (important – bold), `<em>` (emphasis – italic), `<span>` (inline container), `<blockquote>`, `<pre>`, `<code>` | `style` (inline CSS, sparingly) | `<p>Motto: <em>Work, Discipline, Success</em></p>` |
| Unordered list | `<ul>` with `<li>` items | `style="list-style-type: square"` (CSS) | `<ul><li>Sciences</li><li>Arts</li></ul>` |
| Ordered list | `<ol>` with `<li>` | `type="1|A|a|I|i"`, `start="3"`, `reversed` | `<ol type="A"><li>Form 1</li><li>Form 2</li></ol>` |
| Description list | `<dl>`, `<dt>`, `<dd>` | – | Glossaries |
| **Links (hyperlinks)** | `<a>` | **`href`** (destination: absolute URL, relative path, `#anchor`, `mailto:`, `tel:`), `target="_blank"` (new tab), `title` | `<a href="about.html">About us</a>`; `<a href="https://www.minesec.gov.cm" target="_blank">MINESEC</a>`; `<a href="mailto:info@school.cm">E-mail</a>`; `<a href="#contact">Contact</a>` |
| **Images** | `<img>` (void) | **`src`** (path/URL), **`alt`** (text alternative – accessibility/SEO, shown if image fails), `width`, `height` (pixels), `title` | `<img src="images/school.jpg" alt="Main building of GBHS Etoug-Ebe" width="400">` |
| **Tables** | `<table>`, `<caption>`, `<thead>`, `<tbody>`, `<tr>` (row), `<th>` (header cell), `<td>` (data cell) | `border="1"` (legacy; prefer CSS), `colspan`, `rowspan`, `scope="col"` | See worked example |
| Page structure (semantic) | `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<article>`, `<aside>`, `<footer>`, generic `<div>` | `id`, `class` | Layout blocks |
| Media & embeds | `<audio controls src>`, `<video controls width src>`, `<iframe src>` | `controls`, `autoplay`(avoid) | Embedding a Meet/YouTube |
| Forms (preview) | `<form>`, `<input type>`, `<label>`, `<button>` | `type="text|email|number"`, `required`, `min`, `max`, `pattern` (validation – Lesson 25) | `<input type="email" required>` |
| Metadata & style | `<meta>`, `<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">`, `<style>` | – | Basic colours/fonts |
| Special characters | Entities | – | `&amp;` &, `&lt;` <, `&copy;` ©, `&eacute;` é, `&nbsp;` |

**Worked example – "My School" home page (`index.html`):**

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>GBHS Etoug-Ebe – Home</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; margin: 20px; color: #222; }
    header { background: #1B2A49; color: white; padding: 15px; }
    nav a { margin-right: 15px; color: #F2A900; }
    table { border-collapse: collapse; }
    th, td { border: 1px solid #999; padding: 6px 10px; }
    th { background: #EEF1F7; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <h1>Government Bilingual High School Etoug-Ebe</h1>
    <p><em>Work – Discipline – Success</em></p>
  </header>

  <nav>
    <a href="index.html">Home</a>
    <a href="about.html">About us</a>
    <a href="#timetable">Timetable</a>
    <a href="https://www.minesec.gov.cm" target="_blank">MINESEC</a>
  </nav>

  <main>
    <section>
      <h2>Welcome</h2>
      <p>Founded in <strong>1979</strong>, our school in Yaoundé offers the
         <a href="about.html#programmes">Anglophone and Francophone</a> sub-systems
         to more than 3,000 learners.</p>
      <img src="images/school.jpg" alt="Main building with the school flag" width="420">
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Departments</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Sciences</li>
        <li>Arts and Languages</li>
        <li>Computer Science &amp; ICT</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Steps to enrol</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Download the form</li>
        <li>Pay fees by mobile money</li>
        <li>Submit documents at the secretariat</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="timetable">
      <h2>ICT Club timetable</h2>
      <table>
        <caption>Weekly sessions – First Term</caption>
        <tr><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Time</th><th scope="col">Room</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>14:00 – 15:30</td><td>Lab 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Thursday</td><td>14:00 – 15:30</td><td>Lab 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td colspan="3">Saturday sessions announced on the notice board</td></tr>
      </table>
    </section>
  </main>

  <hr>
  <footer>
    <p>&copy; 2026 GBHS Etoug-Ebe · <a href="mailto:ictclub@gbhs-etougebe.cm">ictclub@gbhs-etougebe.cm</a></p>
  </footer>
</body>
</html>
```

### 46.3 Creating and displaying pages with a text editor and browser

**Step sheet:** 1. Create a project folder `myschool` with a sub-folder `images`; copy a photo into `images/school.jpg` (compress to < 300 KB). 2. Open a **plain-text editor**: Notepad (Windows), **Notepad++** or **VS Code** (free, syntax colouring, auto-close tags), gedit/Kate (Linux), TextEdit in plain-text mode (Mac) – **never** Word. 3. Type the document structure; **File ▸ Save As** ▸ `index.html` ▸ "All files" type ▸ encoding UTF-8. 4. Double-click the file (or drag into a browser) – the page opens with a `file:///` URL. 5. **Edit–save–refresh** (F5) cycle: add headings, paragraphs, lists; check after each change. 6. Add the image with `alt`; if it does not display, check the path (relative to the HTML file) and file name case. 7. Create `about.html` with the same head, a heading, a paragraph and a link back to `index.html`; link the two pages; add `id="programmes"` to a section and link to it. 8. Add the table; try `colspan`. 9. Add an external link with `target="_blank"` and a `mailto:` link. 10. Right-click ▸ **Inspect** (F12) to see the DOM and fix errors; use validator.w3.org ▸ "Validate by File Upload" and fix warnings. 11. View on a phone: open from a shared folder/USB or through the lab's local web server (Python: `python -m http.server 8000` in the folder → `http://<lab-ip>:8000`). 12. Optional publish: GitHub Pages or Netlify Drop (free) – the page gets a real URL (link to Lesson 40).

| Common error | Symptom | Fix |
| Unclosed tag (`<p>`, `<li>`, `<table>`) | Layout runs on; everything bold/italic | Close tags; indent; use editor highlighting |
| Wrong image path or case (`Images/School.JPG`) | Broken image icon; alt text shown | Match exact path and case; relative to HTML file |
| Missing quotes on attributes with spaces | Attribute breaks | Always `name="value"` |
| Saved as `.txt` or with Word | Code displayed as text or garbled | Save as `.html` plain text, UTF-8 |
| Accents show as `Ã©` | Wrong encoding | `<meta charset="UTF-8">` and save file as UTF-8 |
| Link opens nothing | `href` typo or file missing | Check target file exists in the same folder |
| Table without borders | Default no borders | CSS `border` or `border="1"` |

> EXAMPLE:
  **From notebook to browser in one lesson.** A learner in Garoua types the "My School" page in Notepad++, saves `index.html`, and sees her heading in Firefox within two minutes. Her image fails to load – the folder is `Images` but she typed `images`; she fixes the case. She adds a table of ICT Club sessions, a `mailto:` link to the club and a link to the GCE Board with `target="_blank"`. The validator flags a missing `alt` – she adds "Learners in the computer lab". Served with `python -m http.server`, the page opens on classmates' phones over the lab Wi-Fi, and she recognises the URL parts from Lesson 40: `http://192.168.1.20:8000/index.html`.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 46.1 – Anatomy of an element**: opening tag with attributes, content, closing tag – colour-coded; a void element beside it. **Figure 46.2 – HTML document tree**: html → head (meta, title) and body (header, nav, main ▸ sections ▸ h2/p/img/ul/table, footer). **Figure 46.3 – Side-by-side**: source code of the worked example and the rendered page with arrows linking each tag to its visual result. **Figure 46.4 – Edit–save–refresh loop** and a "debugging checklist" card; screenshot of the W3C validator and DevTools inspector.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "HTML is a programming language." It is a markup language describing structure; logic comes from JavaScript. "More `<br>` and spaces make layout." Use structural elements and CSS; HTML collapses whitespace. "`alt` is optional." It is essential for accessibility and appears when images fail.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Build your mini-site (90 min, individual, lab).** (1) Create `index.html` following the step sheet – structure, headings, two paragraphs with `<strong>`/`<em>`, an unordered and an ordered list, an image with `alt` and `width`, a 3×3 table with headers and one `colspan`, links (internal page, anchor, external in new tab, `mailto:`), footer with `&copy;`. (2) Create `about.html` (your class/club) linked both ways. (3) Validate both pages; fix all errors. (4) Peer test: a classmate clicks every link and checks the image on a phone; record issues fixed. (5) Extension: add a `<style>` block with two colours and a font; add a `<video>` or `<audio>` element; embed a map `<iframe>`. (6) No-computer alternative: write the HTML for a given page mock-up on paper; "render" a classmate's HTML by drawing what a browser would show; tag-matching cards (tag ↔ purpose ↔ attributes).

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Describe the structure of an HTML document (doctype, html, head, body) and the purpose of `<meta charset>` and `<title>`.
  2. Explain the difference between a tag, an element and an attribute with an example of each; name three void elements.
  3. Write the HTML for: a level-2 heading; a paragraph with one bold word; an ordered list of three steps starting at 5; a link to about.html opening in a new tab; an image with alt text 200 px wide; a 2×2 table with headers.
  4. Explain the role of the `href`, `src` and `alt` attributes and the difference between absolute and relative paths.
  5. List the steps to create a web page in a text editor and display it in a browser; name three common errors and their fixes.
  6. Why should `alt` text always be provided, and why is HTML not called a programming language?
  **Homework:** Complete a three-page personal mini-site (home, hobbies/portfolio, contact) with all the basic elements, validated and zipped (`.zip` with `images/` folder) – submit by e-mail with a proper subject and message (Lesson 39); include a 100-word reflection on the errors you debugged.

